WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:06.799
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Good
morning, everyone.

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:10.733
Good morning and welcome
to the second day of Next.

00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:13.933
So, as you know, we've
been building and operating a

00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:18.600
hyperscale Cloud for a very long
time, and our goal throughout

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.300
this time actually has remained
the same, namely to make it

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.933
possible for you to build great
applications and to be very

00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:31.899
productive as a developer or as
IT while we deliver this on a

00:00:31.899 --> 00:00:35.266
global scale with
unprecedented reliability,

00:00:35.266 --> 00:00:37.000
performance, and security.

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:42.066
And this Cloud powers all of
Google's services, including the

00:00:42.066 --> 00:00:49.733
seven billion user services, so
YouTube, Gmail, Search, and now

00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:54.233
it also powers a GCP,
which itself is a billion user

00:00:54.233 --> 00:01:00.933
application, meaning that every
single day the users of GCP, the

00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:05.866
customers of GCP, connect to
over a billion individual users

00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:07.733
every single day.

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:11.933
And what makes this possible
is that we started from

00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:13.033
first principles.

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:18.066
We designed every element of our
infrastructure so that you could

00:01:18.066 --> 00:01:23.266
be uniquely productive and that
you can enjoy the performance

00:01:23.266 --> 00:01:24.400
that we created.

00:01:26.066 --> 00:01:31.000
Now when designing this from
first principles, you have to go

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.200
and actually optimize
every single element, so from

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.766
efficient data centers to custom
servers to custom networking

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:44.266
gear to a software-defined
global background to specialized

00:01:44.266 --> 00:01:47.033
ASICs for machine learning.

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:50.400
We've been living
this Cloud at scale.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.233
And, in fact, in just the last
three years, we spent almost $30

00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:58.066
billion on capital
expenditures alone.

00:01:59.366 --> 00:02:04.066
Now one example where this
all shows is our network.

00:02:04.066 --> 00:02:07.133
It's probably the largest
global network today.

00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:13.699
Analysts put its traffic at
between 25 and 40% of global

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:16.733
Internet user traffic.

00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:20.766
And as a GCP or G Suite
customer, you benefit from this

00:02:20.766 --> 00:02:26.699
network because your traffic
travels on our private

00:02:26.699 --> 00:02:29.766
ultra-high-speed backbone
for minimum latency.

00:02:29.766 --> 00:02:35.933
And 98% of the time we hand off
your packets directly to the

00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:40.099
end-user ISP because we
interconnect directly with

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:44.033
almost any ISP
everywhere in the world.

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:45.900
So you won't see any congestion.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.666
And fewer handoffs mean more
throughput, lower latency, and

00:02:50.666 --> 00:02:52.033
better security.

00:02:53.633 --> 00:02:58.599
So, in fact, we have a
global network presence in 182

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:01.866
countries or
territories in the world.

00:03:01.866 --> 00:03:05.233
For comparison, the
United Nations today has 193

00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:07.233
Member States.

00:03:08.199 --> 00:03:10.900
And so to carry this traffic to
pretty much everywhere in the

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.366
world, we also
need to cross oceans.

00:03:14.366 --> 00:03:18.666
And so nine years ago Google
became the first non-telecom

00:03:18.666 --> 00:03:21.733
company to build
an undersea cable.

00:03:21.733 --> 00:03:23.266
That was the Unity
cable from the U.S.

00:03:23.266 --> 00:03:24.266
to Japan.

00:03:24.266 --> 00:03:28.066
And since then, we've built
or acquired fiber capacity,

00:03:28.066 --> 00:03:31.466
submarine fiber capacity, pretty
much anywhere in the world so we

00:03:31.466 --> 00:03:35.366
have a redundant backbone
to pretty much any place.

00:03:35.366 --> 00:03:39.766
So, for example, last year we
turned up what today is the

00:03:39.766 --> 00:03:43.400
highest capacity submarine
cable in the world from the U.S.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:44.866
to Asia.

00:03:44.866 --> 00:03:48.833
And in just a few months, we'll
turn up an even higher capacity

00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:50.500
cable between the U.S.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:51.599
and South America.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.000
Now one of the ways we use
this network, of course, is to

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.333
connect GCP
regions to each other.

00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:03.699
Last year we announced
that we're building 11 new

00:04:03.699 --> 00:04:05.233
Cloud regions.

00:04:05.233 --> 00:04:09.666
Of these 11, Oregon and Tokyo
are already live, Singapore and

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:12.766
Norther Virginia will go live
in just the next few months, and

00:04:12.766 --> 00:04:16.166
Sydney and London will
come on shortly after that.

00:04:16.166 --> 00:04:18.333
And today I'm excited to
announce that we are adding

00:04:18.333 --> 00:04:22.666
three more regions to the set,
namely the Netherlands, Canada,

00:04:22.666 --> 00:04:23.666
and California.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.899
And all of these will come
online either this year or next

00:04:33.899 --> 00:04:38.100
and bring the total count of GCP
regions globally to 17 and the

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.833
total number of zones to 50.

00:04:41.466 --> 00:04:45.066
Now our technology
leadership extends from

00:04:45.066 --> 00:04:47.199
hardware to software.

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:51.733
If you think about Containers,
MapReduce, NoSQL, TensorFlow,

00:04:51.733 --> 00:04:56.000
Serverless, Kubernetes, they all
originated at Google and they've

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.533
been powering our services,
including GCP, for a long time.

00:05:01.533 --> 00:05:06.000
And recently we added yet
another of these ground-breaking

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.066
new systems to our lineup so
you can use it on GCP, and I'm

00:05:10.066 --> 00:05:11.366
talking about Cloud Spanner.

00:05:12.866 --> 00:05:16.833
Spanner solves a long-standing
problem in databases.

00:05:16.833 --> 00:05:22.033
Until now, you could either use
a database, a SQL database with

00:05:22.033 --> 00:05:26.800
nice transactional semantics,
but it was really hard to scale

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.666
it beyond one or just a few
machines and very complicated

00:05:30.666 --> 00:05:32.300
and very expensive.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.699
Or you could use a no SQL
system like Bigtable and you get

00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:40.600
infinite scale, horizontal
scalability, but you no longer

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:43.133
have strong consistency
guarantees, and so you're

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:46.566
pushing the complexity
into your application.

00:05:46.566 --> 00:05:50.833
Neither of these two choices
gave you a truly global system

00:05:50.833 --> 00:05:54.666
because your replicas needed to
be pretty close to each other in

00:05:54.666 --> 00:05:57.399
order to get good performance.

00:05:57.399 --> 00:06:00.800
And Cloud Spanner, which is a
globally distributed database

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.000
service, solves these problems.

00:06:03.666 --> 00:06:07.966
So it scales horizontally like
a NoSQL system and thus it can

00:06:07.966 --> 00:06:12.600
take pretty much any transaction
loads, but it also has these

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:18.199
strong consistency guarantees
and the relational SQL semantics

00:06:18.199 --> 00:06:20.166
of traditional databases.

00:06:20.166 --> 00:06:25.399
And as an extra bonus, it can
also be globally distributed

00:06:25.399 --> 00:06:28.600
while managing high performance.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:32.466
We use it for hundreds of
mission critical applications

00:06:32.466 --> 00:06:35.633
inside of Google, including
AdWords, and now it's available

00:06:35.633 --> 00:06:36.500
on GCP as well.

00:06:38.066 --> 00:06:40.566
So how does Spanner do that?

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:42.966
I don't really have time to go
into that but I'll show you

00:06:42.966 --> 00:06:44.300
one example.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:49.266
So Spanner uses a timestamp
service, a highly accurate

00:06:49.266 --> 00:06:54.266
timestamp service, to keep the
copies of your data in sync.

00:06:56.433 --> 00:07:00.399
This timestamp service is
powered by atomic clocks because

00:07:00.399 --> 00:07:02.766
it needs to be really
very highly accurate.

00:07:02.766 --> 00:07:06.899
So that means we literally run
atomic clocks in every one of

00:07:06.899 --> 00:07:10.466
our data centers to
make Spanner possible.

00:07:10.466 --> 00:07:15.033
But with GCP, since Spanner is
a service, you see none of

00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:16.566
that complexity.

00:07:16.566 --> 00:07:21.466
In fact, Cloud Spanner is fully
managed and serverless so that

00:07:21.466 --> 00:07:24.300
you don't have to worry
about machine types, capacity

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.766
planning, replication, software
updates, really anything.

00:07:27.766 --> 00:07:32.233
And that's what we try to
provide with all GCP services,

00:07:32.233 --> 00:07:37.933
the as much value as possible
with as little administration

00:07:37.933 --> 00:07:38.600
as possible.

00:07:39.899 --> 00:07:42.933
And to show you how Cloud
Spanner really works, I am

00:07:42.933 --> 00:07:44.933
pleased to
introduce Greg DeMichillie.

00:07:51.199 --> 00:07:53.500
&gt;&gt; GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Thanks, Urs.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:54.866
Thanks, everyone.

00:07:54.866 --> 00:07:57.666
I hope you like live demos
because this is the first of

00:07:57.666 --> 00:08:01.766
four live GCP demos you're going
to see in the keynote today.

00:08:01.766 --> 00:08:03.666
And I couldn't be more
excited to have Spanner be the

00:08:03.666 --> 00:08:04.333
first one.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.600
To give you a picture of how
amazing Spanner is, let's start

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:11.466
at the beginning, just
creating the database.

00:08:11.466 --> 00:08:14.133
Those of you who have actually
tried to deploy a multi-region

00:08:14.133 --> 00:08:17.300
database that's highly scalable,
highly reliable, and highly

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.033
reliant, know that
there's like a zillion steps.

00:08:20.033 --> 00:08:22.166
You've got to configure
machines, you've got to set up

00:08:22.166 --> 00:08:24.699
replication, you've got to
worry about failover, you've got

00:08:24.699 --> 00:08:28.033
sharding, what happens if I have
network issues, and then there

00:08:28.033 --> 00:08:30.466
is the operational side of
just keeping the thing running,

00:08:30.466 --> 00:08:33.066
having to have whole staffs of
people who do nothing but take

00:08:33.066 --> 00:08:34.000
care of machinery.

00:08:34.799 --> 00:08:37.633
With Cloud Spanner, we
turned that into one machine.

00:08:37.633 --> 00:08:40.866
Robert is going to bring
up the dialogue to do that.

00:08:40.866 --> 00:08:44.466
He names the instance, he
chooses whether he wants it to

00:08:44.466 --> 00:08:47.799
be a single or a multi-regional
database, and then he says how

00:08:47.799 --> 00:08:48.866
many nodes.

00:08:48.866 --> 00:08:51.000
And in this case, he picks
42 because that's always the

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.000
right answer.

00:08:53.966 --> 00:08:54.899
So that's it.

00:08:54.899 --> 00:08:57.866
We've now created a
multi-regional database that has

00:08:57.866 --> 00:08:59.033
amazing capabilities.

00:08:59.033 --> 00:09:01.600
But that's getting started.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.833
Let's look at Spanner in an
actual real-world scale example.

00:09:05.733 --> 00:09:07.566
Imagine you're a ticket seller.

00:09:07.566 --> 00:09:10.566
You want to sell
tickets to events worldwide to

00:09:10.566 --> 00:09:11.866
customers worldwide.

00:09:11.866 --> 00:09:14.633
Now obviously it's pretty
important that you sell a ticket

00:09:14.633 --> 00:09:16.633
once and only once, right?

00:09:16.633 --> 00:09:19.966
And it's also pretty important
that customers get a good

00:09:19.966 --> 00:09:22.733
performance experience wherever
in the world they are buying

00:09:22.733 --> 00:09:24.100
their tickets.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:27.166
In this example, we've deployed
Cloud Spanner into three Google

00:09:27.166 --> 00:09:30.933
regions - U.S., Europe, and Asia
- all connected by that private

00:09:30.933 --> 00:09:33.366
network that Urs told you about.

00:09:33.366 --> 00:09:36.766
We're also running workloads
that emulate customers buying

00:09:36.766 --> 00:09:38.133
billions of tickets.

00:09:38.133 --> 00:09:41.166
In this case, we're selling at
about half a million tickets per

00:09:41.166 --> 00:09:44.200
minute through this system,
all through a system that is

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:48.033
maintaining consistent version
snapshots, full asset support

00:09:48.033 --> 00:09:51.100
for transactions,
full, strong consistency.

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:54.200
That's the power of Spanner
as a distributed base, as a

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:55.266
distributed database.

00:09:55.266 --> 00:09:59.200
Our customers get a low latency
experience; we get good old

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:00.666
traditional SQL consistency.

00:10:01.399 --> 00:10:04.899
Now to prove that this really is
a database of some size, let me

00:10:04.899 --> 00:10:08.166
show you the schema
behind this database.

00:10:08.166 --> 00:10:10.500
You can see it
actually is a SQL database.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.133
There are columns
that are joined together.

00:10:12.133 --> 00:10:15.933
And if you look at that ticket
table, it has three billion rows

00:10:15.933 --> 00:10:17.133
in it.

00:10:17.133 --> 00:10:20.266
So while we're doing this, in
fact, we're running all this

00:10:20.266 --> 00:10:22.466
traffic against a
real SQL database.

00:10:22.466 --> 00:10:26.266
In fact, we can actually run SQL
queries against that database

00:10:26.266 --> 00:10:29.233
while it's being hammered
with all that traffic.

00:10:29.233 --> 00:10:30.500
Here we have a query.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:32.966
And if you look at the bottom
part of the where clause, you

00:10:32.966 --> 00:10:36.033
will see that we are looking
for tickets in the U.S.

00:10:36.033 --> 00:10:40.500
that are still available as of
10:00 PM and how many seats

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:41.433
they have.

00:10:41.433 --> 00:10:44.166
So these are our top five
events with unsold tickets.

00:10:45.233 --> 00:10:46.866
Now what's important
here is what you don't see.

00:10:46.866 --> 00:10:50.333
What's remarkable is how
unremarkable this SQL looks.

00:10:50.333 --> 00:10:53.566
If you have deployed a sharding
system, your SQL is a mess

00:10:53.566 --> 00:10:56.200
because you're trying to have to
do joins across multiple shards

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:57.333
of your database.

00:10:57.333 --> 00:11:00.466
In fact, it's probably not even
human writable SQL when you use

00:11:00.466 --> 00:11:01.500
those systems.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:02.933
Spanner takes care of all that.

00:11:04.833 --> 00:11:06.966
But you know real-world
applications aren't static; they

00:11:06.966 --> 00:11:08.266
change over time.

00:11:08.266 --> 00:11:10.399
What happens when
your needs change?

00:11:10.399 --> 00:11:12.966
Well, first of all, Spanner
lets you make transactionally

00:11:12.966 --> 00:11:17.233
consistent schema updates with
no downtime, which is critically

00:11:17.233 --> 00:11:19.133
important when you're running
systems that your business

00:11:19.133 --> 00:11:20.899
depends on.

00:11:20.899 --> 00:11:23.266
And what happens if you get a
wonderful success and your

00:11:23.266 --> 00:11:24.366
needs grow?

00:11:24.366 --> 00:11:27.600
Well, with a traditional SQL
database, you better put a PO in

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.033
for a brand new server, you
better figure out how to

00:11:30.033 --> 00:11:32.500
configure the new server, you
better figure out how to migrate

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:35.733
the data, you better
figure out how to do cutover.

00:11:35.733 --> 00:11:39.566
Or if I'm doing scale out, well
now I've got to add a whole new

00:11:39.566 --> 00:11:41.933
complex middleware layer
that's incredibly difficult

00:11:41.933 --> 00:11:42.799
to maintain.

00:11:42.799 --> 00:11:44.799
Even that takes time
and adds operational risk.

00:11:45.733 --> 00:11:48.799
In Cloud Spanner, Robert is
going to go back to the console

00:11:48.799 --> 00:11:52.133
and show you how we add
capacity to a Spanner database.

00:11:52.133 --> 00:11:55.333
We simply go from
70 nodes to 99 nodes.

00:11:55.333 --> 00:11:56.166
That's it.

00:11:56.166 --> 00:11:57.600
We've scaled out.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.899
That's the power of a
horizontally scalable

00:11:59.899 --> 00:12:01.033
SQL database.

00:12:07.933 --> 00:12:11.233
And, of course, since we use
Spanner so much internally, we

00:12:11.233 --> 00:12:12.666
made sure it
handles large database.

00:12:12.666 --> 00:12:15.566
Robert is going to switch over
to the monitoring console here

00:12:15.566 --> 00:12:17.766
and what I want you to
look at is that graph on the

00:12:17.766 --> 00:12:19.200
bottom left.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.266
We've been running this
whole demo against an 80

00:12:21.266 --> 00:12:23.933
terabyte database.

00:12:23.933 --> 00:12:26.666
And if you look at the top right
graph, when we loaded the data

00:12:26.666 --> 00:12:30.733
in, we loaded the data at an
average of 800 megabytes per

00:12:30.733 --> 00:12:35.866
second, and we peaked at about a
gig per second of loading data.

00:12:35.866 --> 00:12:36.833
That's amazing.

00:12:36.833 --> 00:12:38.833
That's crazy fast.

00:12:38.833 --> 00:12:39.866
So that's Cloud Spanner.

00:12:40.333 --> 00:12:43.899
You create a highly reliable
performant, fully managed,

00:12:43.899 --> 00:12:46.600
multi-region
database in seconds.

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:48.166
Cloud Spanner is SQL.

00:12:48.166 --> 00:12:51.733
You get interoperability in the
power of SQL queries, you get

00:12:51.733 --> 00:12:55.133
ASIC transactions, plus you get
advanced features like on the

00:12:55.133 --> 00:12:57.233
fly schema changes.

00:12:57.233 --> 00:12:58.899
Cloud Spanner is fully managed.

00:12:58.899 --> 00:13:00.866
We never had to worry
about setting up replication,

00:13:00.866 --> 00:13:02.366
sharding, failover.

00:13:02.366 --> 00:13:05.566
The system actually even tunes
itself automatically over time.

00:13:05.566 --> 00:13:08.633
So, as you use it, it learns
your query patterns and it

00:13:08.633 --> 00:13:12.100
optimizes its own
performance characteristics.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:13.700
And Cloud Spanner
is built for scale.

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:16.766
We've been running our largest
production systems on this

00:13:16.766 --> 00:13:17.433
for years.

00:13:18.033 --> 00:13:20.366
So with that, I'll turn it back
over to Urs for a little bit

00:13:20.366 --> 00:13:22.000
more of the keynote. Thanks.

00:13:28.533 --> 00:13:30.200
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Thank you, Greg.

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:33.566
So if you're used to traditional
databases, it takes a while to

00:13:33.566 --> 00:13:34.700
absorb that.

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:40.899
I just want to summarize because
Spanner is truly something new.

00:13:40.899 --> 00:13:46.399
It is a SQL database, a
traditional SQL database, with

00:13:46.399 --> 00:13:51.266
the same semantics, that just
scales by changing a value on

00:13:51.266 --> 00:13:52.866
the admin interface.

00:13:52.866 --> 00:13:56.033
It scales through thousands of
nodes and has the same strong

00:13:56.033 --> 00:13:59.566
consistency guarantees that
your applications have come to

00:13:59.566 --> 00:14:00.666
depend on.

00:14:00.666 --> 00:14:04.166
And, of course, as you saw, easy
to administer, does replication

00:14:04.166 --> 00:14:08.333
automatically, multiple
locations worldwide, all without

00:14:08.333 --> 00:14:09.433
any setup.

00:14:09.433 --> 00:14:12.399
It has been immensely popular
inside of Google, and so we're

00:14:12.399 --> 00:14:15.399
very, very excited that we
have it now available on GCP.

00:14:16.766 --> 00:14:20.433
Now from databases to compute.

00:14:20.433 --> 00:14:23.799
Many of our customers have
demanding workloads, financial

00:14:23.799 --> 00:14:28.000
risk models, movie rendering,
scientific computing, large ARP

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:29.600
systems, and so on.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:34.366
And so starting today, GCP VMs
will come with up to 64-cores

00:14:34.366 --> 00:14:37.366
and 416 gigabytes of memory.

00:14:37.366 --> 00:14:40.466
And like our other VMs,
these VMs are available as

00:14:40.466 --> 00:14:41.733
pre-emptible VMs.

00:14:41.733 --> 00:14:45.233
So if you have some time or
flexibility in your computation,

00:14:45.233 --> 00:14:48.833
you can use them for batch
workloads and get much

00:14:48.833 --> 00:14:51.299
lower prices.

00:14:51.299 --> 00:14:52.466
We're not stopping here.

00:14:52.466 --> 00:14:55.633
So, later this year, you will
see even higher core counts and

00:14:55.633 --> 00:14:57.966
memory sizes of a
terabyte or more.

00:14:59.566 --> 00:15:03.399
In fact, when it comes to
hardware innovation and to

00:15:03.399 --> 00:15:07.100
optimizing the hardware for the
best performance in the Cloud,

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:10.266
we've been working very
closely with industry partners

00:15:10.266 --> 00:15:15.733
throughout the industry to
specialize these systems and to

00:15:15.733 --> 00:15:19.333
accelerate the
innovation in hardware.

00:15:19.333 --> 00:15:21.733
One of these
partners of course is Intel.

00:15:21.733 --> 00:15:25.566
Please welcome RaeJeanne
Skillearn from Intel to talk

00:15:25.566 --> 00:15:27.833
about our
partnership. RaeJeanne.

00:15:35.833 --> 00:15:36.799
&gt;&gt; RAEJEANNE SKILLEARN: Hello.

00:15:36.799 --> 00:15:38.033
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Thank you
for joining us, RaeJeanne.

00:15:38.033 --> 00:15:39.066
It's great to have you here.

00:15:39.366 --> 00:15:40.933
&gt;&gt; RAEJEANNE SKILLEARN:
Yes. Thank you.

00:15:40.933 --> 00:15:43.433
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: We started to
work together quite a long time.

00:15:43.433 --> 00:15:46.100
In fact, I don't quite remember
even how long ago it was.

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:47.200
Can you tell us
about the history?

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:48.566
&gt;&gt; RAEJEANNE SKILLEARN: Yeah.

00:15:48.566 --> 00:15:52.566
It actually started in 2003
with our Intel Core 2 processor.

00:15:52.566 --> 00:15:55.733
Google was able to give us
in-depth performance analysis

00:15:55.733 --> 00:15:59.533
and real-world benchmarks and
enabled us to tune our processor

00:15:59.533 --> 00:16:01.233
for their unique workload.

00:16:01.233 --> 00:16:05.066
Fast forward to 2008, we started
working on the Intel Xeon

00:16:05.066 --> 00:16:07.766
processor, the 5600 at the time.

00:16:07.766 --> 00:16:11.100
And since then, we have
optimized six generations of

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.866
Xeon processors specifically
for Google's environment.

00:16:15.166 --> 00:16:19.500
Now in November of 2016, we
took that collaboration and we

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:21.033
expanded it.

00:16:21.033 --> 00:16:24.833
Your Diane Greene and our Diane
Bryant announced a strategic

00:16:24.833 --> 00:16:27.033
alliance between
our two companies.

00:16:27.033 --> 00:16:30.433
We are now collaborating on
Hybrid Cloud orchestration,

00:16:30.433 --> 00:16:34.299
security, machine and deep
learning, and IoT edge to

00:16:34.299 --> 00:16:35.200
Cloud solutions.

00:16:36.433 --> 00:16:37.466
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: That's right.

00:16:37.466 --> 00:16:41.799
We have had six generations
of custom processors and that

00:16:41.799 --> 00:16:42.933
required a lot of work.

00:16:42.933 --> 00:16:44.899
We actually
learned a lot from that.

00:16:44.899 --> 00:16:48.533
Can you tell us how we worked
together on this and how this

00:16:48.533 --> 00:16:49.666
actually works in practice?

00:16:49.666 --> 00:16:50.333
&gt;&gt; RAEJEANNE SKILLEARN: I can.

00:16:50.333 --> 00:16:51.566
And it starts early.

00:16:51.566 --> 00:16:54.066
It starts in the absolute
earliest phases of our

00:16:54.066 --> 00:16:57.000
architecture and CPU
process development.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:00.299
We take the Google feedback
every step of the way and we

00:17:00.299 --> 00:17:03.966
incorporate it and we iterate
and modify our processors.

00:17:03.966 --> 00:17:06.433
We know that to truly create
the best technology that is

00:17:06.433 --> 00:17:10.266
performance optimized, it really
relies on the software as well.

00:17:10.266 --> 00:17:13.700
That's why both of our companies
are investing in the TensorFlow

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:16.566
and Kubernetes
open source projects.

00:17:16.566 --> 00:17:19.599
Intel is actually going to
contribute code to both of those

00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:22.733
projects so we'll dramatically
improve the performance of both

00:17:22.733 --> 00:17:26.799
Kubernetes and TensorFlow on our
Intel Xeon processors and our

00:17:26.799 --> 00:17:28.533
Intel Xeon Phi processors.

00:17:29.733 --> 00:17:34.666
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: So three weeks
ago we launched the newest

00:17:34.666 --> 00:17:39.166
server, Intel Xeon server
processor codenamed Skylake on

00:17:39.166 --> 00:17:43.466
GCP, but yet you can't buy this
processor anywhere and Intel is

00:17:43.466 --> 00:17:46.966
not planning to launch
it for quite a while.

00:17:46.966 --> 00:17:48.066
&gt;&gt; RAEJEANNE
SKILLEARN: For a while.

00:17:48.066 --> 00:17:49.799
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: How
does that work?

00:17:49.799 --> 00:17:51.400
&gt;&gt; RAEJEANNE SKILLEANER:
Well, first I would like to

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:54.633
congratulate Google on being
the absolute first to have Cloud

00:17:54.633 --> 00:17:57.966
services on our next generation
Xeon processor, Skylake.

00:17:58.633 --> 00:18:01.200
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Great.
Thank you. Thank you.

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:03.766
&gt;&gt; RAEJEANNE SKILLEANER: There
was a trick to doing this.

00:18:03.766 --> 00:18:06.366
First we had to accelerate
production readiness of a

00:18:06.366 --> 00:18:09.133
targeted set of features that
are a little bit different than

00:18:09.133 --> 00:18:12.299
our broad feature set, all the
systems in the software that we

00:18:12.299 --> 00:18:15.366
have to qualify and validate for
the broad general availability.

00:18:16.133 --> 00:18:18.966
Intel and Google started an
early definition on a custom

00:18:18.966 --> 00:18:22.566
skew all the way through
significant joint investment in

00:18:22.566 --> 00:18:25.400
the validation of those
systems into production in

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:26.733
your environment.

00:18:26.733 --> 00:18:31.766
It was a team effort many steps
of the way, at massive scale and

00:18:31.766 --> 00:18:33.666
in a very aggressive
timeline for both of us.

00:18:34.066 --> 00:18:35.900
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Well thank you
for being such a great partner.

00:18:35.900 --> 00:18:37.400
We really enjoyed
working with you.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:37.966
&gt;&gt; RAEJEANNE
SKILLEARN: Thank you.

00:18:37.966 --> 00:18:38.466
We enjoy it.

00:18:38.466 --> 00:18:39.333
We look forward to tomorrow.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:45.599
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: We're very happy
that our customers benefit from

00:18:45.599 --> 00:18:48.900
this decade of collaboration
between us and Intel.

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:52.599
Because we're pushing the
envelope in so many directions

00:18:52.599 --> 00:18:57.566
on performance that we have
to really, really work very

00:18:57.566 --> 00:18:59.000
differently with vendors.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:03.200
Skylake offers great performance
for compute-intensive workloads.

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:07.599
We are very, very happy that
Skylake is available at first on

00:19:07.599 --> 00:19:08.333
Google Cloud.

00:19:09.733 --> 00:19:13.133
Now let's go from
performance to economics.

00:19:13.133 --> 00:19:16.099
Clouds, as you know, are
supposed to be elastic and free

00:19:16.099 --> 00:19:21.233
from capex or capacity planning,
yet some Cloud providers force

00:19:21.233 --> 00:19:26.766
you to pay up front for three
years to get the best price.

00:19:26.766 --> 00:19:30.500
But if you have to buy a VM for
three years, then how is that

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:33.099
better than buying your
own server for three years?

00:19:33.099 --> 00:19:35.066
There's no flexibility.

00:19:35.066 --> 00:19:39.233
And, in fact, a recent study
showed that Cloud users waste on

00:19:39.233 --> 00:19:45.366
average 45% of their spend on
resources that they bought but

00:19:45.366 --> 00:19:46.033
can't use.

00:19:47.966 --> 00:19:50.066
That's caused by many factors.

00:19:50.066 --> 00:19:53.366
One is these three year leases
that force you to predict your

00:19:53.366 --> 00:19:54.366
future perfectly.

00:19:54.366 --> 00:19:57.366
And let's face it;
none of us can do that.

00:19:57.366 --> 00:20:00.700
We end up with stranded
resources that we actually

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:01.966
can't use.

00:20:01.966 --> 00:20:05.166
Similarly, we are forced to
buy - like on premise - we are

00:20:05.166 --> 00:20:09.633
forced to buy servers in
fixed sizes, and so we end up

00:20:09.633 --> 00:20:13.166
overbuying on some dimension,
and we still pay for it even

00:20:13.166 --> 00:20:14.700
though we can't use it.

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:17.566
And last but not least, you pay
for the full hour even when your

00:20:17.566 --> 00:20:19.866
test run runs for
only ten minutes.

00:20:19.866 --> 00:20:22.466
So you pay for compute time
that you're not even using.

00:20:22.466 --> 00:20:25.966
All of that adds
up to 45% waste.

00:20:27.166 --> 00:20:30.566
Now we believe that it should be
easy to get the best price, and

00:20:30.566 --> 00:20:33.166
so we have solved
these sources of waste.

00:20:33.166 --> 00:20:36.766
In fact, in 2014 we introduced
automatic sustained use

00:20:36.766 --> 00:20:39.733
discounts that give
you discounts without any

00:20:39.733 --> 00:20:40.900
long-term agreement.

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:45.000
So as soon as you use a VM for
more than a quarter of a month,

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:46.700
you start seeing savings.

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:49.666
And if you use the VM for
the entire month, you get a

00:20:49.666 --> 00:20:52.000
30% discount.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:57.066
But you're still free to
stop using a VM at any time.

00:20:57.066 --> 00:21:00.266
So sustained use discounts
brought the Cloud back

00:21:00.266 --> 00:21:01.266
into Cloud.

00:21:01.266 --> 00:21:03.866
You can change your mind
at any time but you still get

00:21:03.866 --> 00:21:04.566
great prices.

00:21:05.933 --> 00:21:10.333
And you don't need to accept
VMs whose shapes come in powers

00:21:10.333 --> 00:21:11.666
of two.

00:21:11.666 --> 00:21:15.866
So with other providers, if your
application needs say 20 cores

00:21:15.866 --> 00:21:20.900
and 50 gigabytes of RAM, you
might have to buy the next

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:25.966
larger machine size that might
have 40 cores and 160 gigabytes

00:21:25.966 --> 00:21:26.933
of RAM.

00:21:26.933 --> 00:21:30.833
And so you end up paying
literally twice the resources

00:21:30.833 --> 00:21:31.900
that you need.

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:36.366
But not on GCP, because with
custom machine types, you can

00:21:36.366 --> 00:21:39.666
dial in exactly the
configuration you need so you

00:21:39.666 --> 00:21:43.000
pay for exactly the
resources you need.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:48.599
Today, over 20% of our core
hours on GCP are for custom

00:21:48.599 --> 00:21:52.833
machine types and our users
save an average of 19% through

00:21:52.833 --> 00:21:53.533
the customization.

00:21:54.866 --> 00:21:57.766
And we even help you
save money with our right

00:21:57.766 --> 00:21:58.966
sizing recommendations.

00:21:58.966 --> 00:22:02.566
That's a service that looks at
your memory and CPU usage and

00:22:02.566 --> 00:22:06.400
then suggests the best
virtual machine size.

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:09.866
And last but not least, GCP
has permanent billing for VMs.

00:22:09.866 --> 00:22:15.266
So if you use a VM for 11
minutes, you pay for 11 minutes.

00:22:15.266 --> 00:22:17.833
It seems pretty logical.

00:22:17.833 --> 00:22:23.233
So GCP and only GCP is
truly an elastic Cloud.

00:22:23.233 --> 00:22:28.099
You only buy what you need,
you only pay for it when you

00:22:28.099 --> 00:22:31.533
actually use it, you get
automatic discount if you use it

00:22:31.533 --> 00:22:34.200
for extended periods of time,
and we even alert you when you

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:36.066
appear to be wasting resources.

00:22:36.066 --> 00:22:40.633
And when you put all of this
together, you save an average of

00:22:40.633 --> 00:22:44.700
60% relative to what you
would pay on other Clouds.

00:22:46.166 --> 00:22:49.733
So given the complexity of Cloud
pricing we just went through,

00:22:49.733 --> 00:22:53.833
it's not surprising that the
same study I quoted says that

00:22:53.833 --> 00:22:59.299
53% of Cloud users say that
optimizing and controlling spend

00:22:59.299 --> 00:23:02.566
is their top
problem in the Cloud.

00:23:02.566 --> 00:23:04.166
But not with GCP.

00:23:04.166 --> 00:23:08.000
Because with GCP, you don't need
to create an entire new ministry

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:12.766
in your company just to get the
best price because our flexible

00:23:12.766 --> 00:23:16.900
pricing structure lets you enjoy
a Cloud as it was meant to be -

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:20.433
on demand, pay as you go,
paying only for what you need.

00:23:22.233 --> 00:23:25.366
But today it gets even better
because we're introducing

00:23:25.366 --> 00:23:29.166
another way for you to save
- committed use discounts.

00:23:29.166 --> 00:23:32.200
So in exchange for a one or
three-year commitment, you

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:36.066
receive a discount of
up to 57% billed monthly,

00:23:36.066 --> 00:23:37.500
no upfront payment.

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:41.466
But these are not inflexible
reserved instances that lock you

00:23:41.466 --> 00:23:47.366
into a particular instance type
or family that force you to pay

00:23:47.366 --> 00:23:48.533
up front. No.

00:23:48.533 --> 00:23:52.733
They only commit you to an
overall volume for your compute

00:23:52.733 --> 00:23:54.066
and memory.

00:23:54.066 --> 00:23:58.599
So you're not locked into
any particular VM size.

00:23:58.599 --> 00:24:01.466
You can change individual
machine types and VM shapes

00:24:01.466 --> 00:24:02.166
at will.

00:24:02.166 --> 00:24:03.500
You can change
the number of VMs.

00:24:03.500 --> 00:24:07.666
You're only committing to
the aggregate volume and not

00:24:07.666 --> 00:24:08.333
the details.

00:24:09.766 --> 00:24:13.466
And if you're not sure how much
commitment to make, you can

00:24:13.466 --> 00:24:19.166
start smaller because you still
get sustained use discounts and

00:24:19.166 --> 00:24:22.633
all the other benefits on
any of the usage above your

00:24:22.633 --> 00:24:23.833
committed usage.

00:24:23.833 --> 00:24:29.700
And so you're not facing a huge
unit cost cliff when you exceed

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:32.466
your forecast.

00:24:32.466 --> 00:24:36.333
Because we believe that when
you move to the Cloud, capacity

00:24:36.333 --> 00:24:39.533
planning and cost planning
should really become a distant

00:24:39.533 --> 00:24:42.966
memory and not your
number one headache.

00:24:42.966 --> 00:24:46.299
So on GCP you are saving money
automatically with no regrets,

00:24:46.299 --> 00:24:50.633
no spreadsheets, and no PhD in
economics needed to manage

00:24:50.633 --> 00:24:51.299
it all.

00:24:52.599 --> 00:24:57.466
In fact, one way where you see
this flexibility is our GPUs.

00:24:57.466 --> 00:25:00.933
Many of our customers
already GPUs for a variety of

00:25:00.933 --> 00:25:04.366
applications to speed up
simulations, transcoding, deep

00:25:04.366 --> 00:25:07.299
learning, computational
chemistry, finance, and

00:25:07.299 --> 00:25:08.533
many more.

00:25:08.533 --> 00:25:14.166
But on GCP, you can add a GPU to
any VM configuration and start

00:25:14.166 --> 00:25:17.400
it up in under a minute, much
faster than our competitors.

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:21.766
So that means that if you want a
GPU with lots of memory, you can

00:25:21.766 --> 00:25:22.900
get that.

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:27.233
Or if you want a GPU with little
memory but lots of cores and an

00:25:27.233 --> 00:25:32.666
SSD, you can get that, and you
don't waste money on your GPU

00:25:32.666 --> 00:25:36.566
because you have to buy
some other stuff with it.

00:25:36.566 --> 00:25:40.500
And, of course, GPUs also have
per minute billing so you don't

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:43.566
end up paying when
you're not actually using it.

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:47.966
So to help you learn more about
this, I want to introduce one of

00:25:47.966 --> 00:25:52.000
our customers who is using
GPUs as a critical part of

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:53.299
their business.

00:25:53.299 --> 00:25:56.700
Please welcome Ashok
Belani from Schlumberger.

00:26:06.366 --> 00:26:07.266
Hi, Ashok.

00:26:07.266 --> 00:26:08.033
Great to have you here.

00:26:08.333 --> 00:26:09.133
&gt;&gt; ASHOK BELANI: Thank
you for having me.

00:26:10.033 --> 00:26:11.933
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: So Schlumberger
moved to Google platform

00:26:11.933 --> 00:26:13.733
last year.

00:26:13.733 --> 00:26:16.066
What drove you to adopt Google?

00:26:17.099 --> 00:26:19.500
&gt;&gt; ASHOK BELANI: At
Schlumberger, we've been leaders

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:23.433
in high performance
computing for many decades.

00:26:23.433 --> 00:26:28.000
You see the size of our compute
clusters growing on the slide.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:32.933
We bought the first grade back
I think in the mid '80s and we

00:26:32.933 --> 00:26:36.400
moved to massively
powered PCs in the '90s.

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:40.566
And then we were actually one of
the first companies to jump onto

00:26:40.566 --> 00:26:47.433
very high GPU to CPU
ratios back in 2007 or 2008.

00:26:47.433 --> 00:26:50.166
What we think is that we can
go to the next level of compute

00:26:50.166 --> 00:26:53.500
possibilities working
together with Google.

00:26:54.033 --> 00:26:57.333
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: So you clearly
use massive amounts of compute.

00:26:57.333 --> 00:27:00.033
Tell us what you're
actually doing with compute

00:27:00.033 --> 00:27:00.799
and those GPUs.

00:27:01.566 --> 00:27:04.700
&gt;&gt; ASHOK BELANI: So we are
looking in the subsurface for

00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:09.066
our geologists who are exploring
for oil and gas deposits.

00:27:09.066 --> 00:27:12.666
So we look very deep into the
subsurface, tens of thousands

00:27:12.666 --> 00:27:13.766
of feet.

00:27:13.766 --> 00:27:18.666
We use acoustic data and
dynamic data of different kinds.

00:27:18.666 --> 00:27:23.299
Here you see on the slide behind
me a vessel which has a huge

00:27:23.299 --> 00:27:26.933
spread of sensors which
are being pulled behind it.

00:27:26.933 --> 00:27:30.900
There are millions of sensors on
a spread which is ten kilometers

00:27:30.900 --> 00:27:32.466
by two kilometers.

00:27:32.466 --> 00:27:37.033
Sometimes we use two of these
boats which would cover half the

00:27:37.033 --> 00:27:38.966
surface area of San Francisco.

00:27:38.966 --> 00:27:41.666
And, as you see, the amount of
data that we generate has been

00:27:41.666 --> 00:27:43.966
increasing over the
years significantly.

00:27:43.966 --> 00:27:47.233
And then we use high performance
computing to create images of

00:27:47.233 --> 00:27:49.799
the subsurface for
our exploration efforts.

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:52.633
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: So tell us
how your experience has been.

00:27:52.633 --> 00:27:54.533
How has it been
working with Google?

00:27:54.533 --> 00:27:56.099
And tell us actually
what we're seeing here.

00:27:56.599 --> 00:28:01.133
&gt;&gt; ASHOK BELANI: So we see an
image here which actually has

00:28:01.133 --> 00:28:05.633
been - the data has been
acquired over about five months.

00:28:05.633 --> 00:28:09.099
This data was actually processed
in Google over a period of

00:28:09.099 --> 00:28:10.233
three months.

00:28:10.233 --> 00:28:14.599
And you see the subsurface which
is actually 20,000 feet into the

00:28:14.599 --> 00:28:19.566
ground and it is 50,000 square
kilometers off the shore of

00:28:19.566 --> 00:28:24.533
Mexico - bigger than
the size of Switzerland.

00:28:25.066 --> 00:28:28.666
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Our
mountains are taller.

00:28:28.666 --> 00:28:29.666
No need to go on the ground.

00:28:32.066 --> 00:28:34.000
&gt;&gt; ASHOK BELANI: I think the
interesting thing about working

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:38.533
with the Google Cloud is that we
are able to create basically a

00:28:38.533 --> 00:28:42.166
cluster which is suited for that
particular problem because of

00:28:42.166 --> 00:28:44.599
the algorithms we are going
to use on that problem.

00:28:44.599 --> 00:28:47.133
Within a matter of minutes, we
are able to mix the CPUs, GPUs,

00:28:47.133 --> 00:28:52.799
high GPU ratios, memory close
to the processor the way we need

00:28:52.799 --> 00:28:55.299
for particular algorithms
to work in a very good way.

00:28:55.299 --> 00:28:59.099
This is a big advantage
that we get out of Google.

00:29:00.133 --> 00:29:02.799
But I would say another
advantage is Urs Holzle himself.

00:29:02.799 --> 00:29:04.533
I think Google should be
very proud of having someone

00:29:04.533 --> 00:29:05.200
like this.

00:29:07.333 --> 00:29:10.033
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Okay. Yeah.

00:29:10.033 --> 00:29:11.500
Let that be
stricken from the record.

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:13.000
That was not in the script.

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:14.400
&gt;&gt; ASHOK BELANI: That
wasn't in the script.

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:18.200
We certainly cooperate very well
with the engineers in Google.

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:22.066
We actually created a center
here in Menlo Park so that our

00:29:22.066 --> 00:29:24.966
engineers could work together
with Google very closely.

00:29:24.966 --> 00:29:32.599
And we are only at say 10-15% of
the way on this journey to make

00:29:32.599 --> 00:29:38.466
Cloud very efficient
on our applications.

00:29:38.466 --> 00:29:42.266
We think we will be able to
weave in things like big data

00:29:42.266 --> 00:29:47.566
type of technologies or
analytics or machine learning.

00:29:47.566 --> 00:29:50.200
In the future, they will be
woven into our applications and

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:52.833
I think we will definitely be
able to achieve the next level

00:29:52.833 --> 00:29:54.700
of computing in oil and gas.

00:29:54.700 --> 00:29:58.466
And I think we serve all the
oil companies in the world.

00:29:58.466 --> 00:30:02.333
So as we go on this journey,
they will come together with us.

00:30:03.433 --> 00:30:04.333
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE:
Thank you very much.

00:30:04.333 --> 00:30:05.433
It's really been
great working with you.

00:30:05.433 --> 00:30:05.966
&gt;&gt; ASHOK BELANI:
Thank you very much.

00:30:05.966 --> 00:30:06.833
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Thank you, Ashok.

00:30:11.466 --> 00:30:12.633
All right.

00:30:12.633 --> 00:30:18.266
So when you use Google, you use
the same security infrastructure

00:30:18.266 --> 00:30:20.400
that Google uses.

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:23.933
And we've been investing an
enormous amount of effort into

00:30:23.933 --> 00:30:25.066
that security.

00:30:25.066 --> 00:30:27.166
You can see an overview
of the many layers here.

00:30:27.166 --> 00:30:29.566
I don't really have time
to talk about that today.

00:30:29.566 --> 00:30:32.333
Today I would like to show
you just a few highlights.

00:30:32.333 --> 00:30:36.566
But we have a detailed security
design, white paper, and we have

00:30:36.566 --> 00:30:41.400
a one-hour session that do
real justice to this topic.

00:30:42.833 --> 00:30:46.200
So we put a lot of effort
into security starting with

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:47.299
physical security.

00:30:47.299 --> 00:30:50.733
For example, in this single
Google data center campus we

00:30:50.733 --> 00:30:56.933
have over 175 security guards
on staff 24/7 in addition to

00:30:56.933 --> 00:31:00.900
countless cameras, motion
sensors, iris scanners, and

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:02.433
so on.

00:31:02.433 --> 00:31:06.066
Many of our locations are not
individual data centers but data

00:31:06.066 --> 00:31:07.666
center campuses.

00:31:07.666 --> 00:31:12.433
And so this very high level of
physical security is amortized

00:31:12.433 --> 00:31:15.733
over hundreds of
thousands of servers.

00:31:15.733 --> 00:31:20.133
And thus on GCP,
low prices don't mean low

00:31:20.133 --> 00:31:21.466
security standards.

00:31:22.833 --> 00:31:28.633
In fact, to protect the security
of our hardware, we put a

00:31:28.633 --> 00:31:32.533
security chip on all our new
machines to serve as the basis

00:31:32.533 --> 00:31:35.566
of trust for that
machine's identity.

00:31:35.566 --> 00:31:38.833
So this is a custom chip
designed by Google so we know

00:31:38.833 --> 00:31:43.966
exactly what it does and it
helps us protect servers against

00:31:43.966 --> 00:31:46.933
tampering even at the bios
level; or in this particular

00:31:46.933 --> 00:31:52.599
example, it helps protect the
bios of the networking card that

00:31:52.599 --> 00:31:53.266
we built.

00:31:54.433 --> 00:31:59.166
The chip, in fact, is so small
that I'm actually wearing one on

00:31:59.166 --> 00:32:02.033
my earring here.

00:32:02.033 --> 00:32:04.033
So here it is.

00:32:06.666 --> 00:32:14.533
So now you know that you
are watching the authentic

00:32:14.533 --> 00:32:16.400
GCP keynote.

00:32:21.266 --> 00:32:25.633
So this hardware chip helps
us authenticate the hardware.

00:32:25.633 --> 00:32:28.266
And then on top of that, it
helps us authenticate the

00:32:28.266 --> 00:32:29.866
services that we run.

00:32:29.866 --> 00:32:33.200
When services call each other,
so the services that implement

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:38.033
GCP, they must mutually prove
their identity to each other and

00:32:38.033 --> 00:32:42.900
use certificates for that and
the binaries are signed and

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:46.599
cryptographically signed so that
we can verify we are running the

00:32:46.599 --> 00:32:48.200
right binary.

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:52.266
And then on the storage side,
GCP's durable storage services

00:32:52.266 --> 00:32:57.400
encrypt all data before it is
written to the physical media.

00:32:57.400 --> 00:33:02.000
And on the networking side, all
Internet traffic from and to G

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:06.700
Suite or GCP are protected with
strong encryptions and multiple

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:08.900
layers of service protection.

00:33:10.633 --> 00:33:13.599
Now that covers
the data center side.

00:33:13.599 --> 00:33:18.200
But a system is only as
secure as its user accounts.

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:22.533
Today, phishing probably is the
number one security problem for

00:33:22.533 --> 00:33:26.166
enterprises, meaning someone is
trying to trick your users into

00:33:26.166 --> 00:33:33.099
providing their password and
perhaps their one-time token.

00:33:33.099 --> 00:33:36.766
But your G Suite and GCP account
is already protected by a

00:33:36.766 --> 00:33:40.599
sophisticated abuse detection
system to thwart those attackers

00:33:40.599 --> 00:33:43.200
that are trying to guess your
password or trying to use a

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:44.866
stolen password.

00:33:44.866 --> 00:33:48.633
But with our optional
phishing-resistant second

00:33:48.633 --> 00:33:54.166
factor, we can provide a very
strong defense against phishing.

00:33:54.166 --> 00:33:58.766
No other Cloud today provides
you this protection against what

00:33:58.766 --> 00:34:02.099
is probably the number one
security problem in enterprises.

00:34:03.299 --> 00:34:07.033
For further end-to-end security,
we also ensure that the user's

00:34:07.033 --> 00:34:08.933
client device is secure.

00:34:08.933 --> 00:34:14.133
So Chrome, Chromulus, and
Android are designed ground-up

00:34:14.133 --> 00:34:17.000
for security, all featured
Cloud management and frequent

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:19.400
over-the-air security updates.

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:22.866
And our client operating
systems feature a hardened boot.

00:34:22.866 --> 00:34:27.166
So they actually have a chip
similar to this to verify that

00:34:27.166 --> 00:34:29.133
they're booting
the correct software.

00:34:29.133 --> 00:34:33.633
They have encryption of storage
by default and enterprise

00:34:33.633 --> 00:34:35.699
grade management.

00:34:35.699 --> 00:34:39.933
And with such a strong
security stack and nearly zero

00:34:39.933 --> 00:34:43.733
administration cost and a wide
range of models to choose from,

00:34:43.733 --> 00:34:47.800
it's no surprise that Chrome
Books outsold Macs last year.

00:34:50.433 --> 00:34:52.599
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Yes, thank you.

00:34:52.599 --> 00:34:55.666
So today we are announcing
several new security features

00:34:55.666 --> 00:34:57.733
that we're adding
to GCP and G Suite.

00:34:57.733 --> 00:35:01.466
First, some tools to
let you secure your data.

00:35:01.466 --> 00:35:05.633
Data loss prevention is an API
now available that lets you

00:35:05.633 --> 00:35:09.599
discover PII and other sensitive
data in your content and take

00:35:09.599 --> 00:35:12.233
appropriate actions
such as redacting it.

00:35:12.233 --> 00:35:14.266
You will see a demo in a minute.

00:35:14.266 --> 00:35:18.566
The engine behind this API is
the same engine that powers the

00:35:18.566 --> 00:35:21.833
data loss prevention feature
in Gmail and Drive, so you get

00:35:21.833 --> 00:35:24.266
consistent results everywhere.

00:35:24.266 --> 00:35:27.666
Next, Cloud Key
Management Service is now

00:35:27.666 --> 00:35:28.766
generally available.

00:35:28.766 --> 00:35:32.800
It lets you manage encryption
for your Cloud services because

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:37.766
Cloud KMS protects your data and
secrets that are stored at rest

00:35:37.766 --> 00:35:42.599
and it can automatically
rotate your keys for you.

00:35:44.466 --> 00:35:48.300
And then we also added tools
that let you safely access that

00:35:48.300 --> 00:35:50.866
secured content
once it's been secured.

00:35:50.866 --> 00:35:54.733
The first one is the
Identity Aware Proxy or IAP.

00:35:54.733 --> 00:35:59.400
It is available in beta and it
enables you to configure secure

00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:01.900
controlled access
to your applications.

00:36:01.900 --> 00:36:06.966
So you can enforce a who can
see what access control at the

00:36:06.966 --> 00:36:08.433
application layer.

00:36:08.433 --> 00:36:12.099
So you don't need client
software, remote access VPNs,

00:36:12.099 --> 00:36:14.166
firewalls,
network configurations.

00:36:14.166 --> 00:36:17.266
You just deploy IAP with a
single click and you're going to

00:36:17.266 --> 00:36:18.933
see that in a second.

00:36:18.933 --> 00:36:23.833
So IAP acts as a smart
front-end in front of all of

00:36:23.833 --> 00:36:25.699
your Cloud applications.

00:36:25.699 --> 00:36:29.633
It's built on top of the GCP
load balancer so you benefit

00:36:29.633 --> 00:36:33.500
from the transport security and
the scalability of the Google

00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:35.966
front-end service.

00:36:35.966 --> 00:36:40.233
And last but not least, security
key enforcement is now available

00:36:40.233 --> 00:36:44.266
to GCP users as part
of G Suite Enterprise.

00:36:44.266 --> 00:36:50.833
So this feature lets you enforce
security key use for all members

00:36:50.833 --> 00:36:52.066
of your domain.

00:36:52.066 --> 00:36:55.800
That means that all of your
applications - access to all of

00:36:55.800 --> 00:36:59.166
your applications is now
strongly phishing-resistant.

00:36:59.166 --> 00:37:03.300
Here to demonstrate some
of these features are Greg

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:04.900
DeMichillie and team.

00:37:10.166 --> 00:37:10.900
&gt;&gt; GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Thanks again, Urs.

00:37:12.833 --> 00:37:16.133
You know, today the task of
taking a corporate application

00:37:16.133 --> 00:37:19.033
and making it available to
your employees outside of your

00:37:19.033 --> 00:37:21.599
network is just painful.

00:37:21.599 --> 00:37:24.833
You've got to set up VPNs
and nobody likes those.

00:37:24.833 --> 00:37:25.633
They're hard to set up.

00:37:25.633 --> 00:37:27.099
They're hard to configure.

00:37:27.099 --> 00:37:29.400
And in the end, you end up with
this perimeter-based security,

00:37:29.400 --> 00:37:33.333
right, with a semi-hard exterior
and a soft underbelly, which

00:37:33.333 --> 00:37:35.966
isn't nearly as
effective as modern device

00:37:35.966 --> 00:37:38.699
multi-factor authentication.

00:37:38.699 --> 00:37:40.733
So we're going to give you an
example of how easy it is to use

00:37:40.733 --> 00:37:44.199
the Identity Aware Proxy to take
a typical corporate enterprise

00:37:44.199 --> 00:37:46.666
app and make it
available to your users.

00:37:46.666 --> 00:37:48.866
Now the specific app we're
going to use in this case is as

00:37:48.866 --> 00:37:49.966
enterprise as it comes.

00:37:49.966 --> 00:37:52.099
It's Oracle E-Business
Suite running on Google

00:37:52.099 --> 00:37:53.933
Cloud platform.

00:37:53.933 --> 00:37:57.099
And as a company, we want our
users to access this application

00:37:57.099 --> 00:37:59.766
whether they are in the office
or they are out on the road.

00:37:59.766 --> 00:38:02.133
So let's see how simple
that is to do with IAP.

00:38:02.466 --> 00:38:05.300
Neil is going to click
the button to turn on IAP.

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:08.766
He's then going to tell us what
domain he wants to publish that

00:38:08.766 --> 00:38:10.400
application for.

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:14.000
And now users in that domain
and only that domain can access

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:15.400
the application.

00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:16.266
So let's test it out.

00:38:16.266 --> 00:38:19.000
He's going to flip
over to a browser tab.

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:22.099
Now the first thing is there
is no VPN software installed on

00:38:22.099 --> 00:38:22.766
this machine.

00:38:22.766 --> 00:38:24.366
We are just
navigating to the URL.

00:38:25.300 --> 00:38:27.766
Now first he's going to try his
personal Gmail account which has

00:38:27.766 --> 00:38:30.266
not been authorized
for this application.

00:38:30.266 --> 00:38:32.666
Sure enough, he is denied.

00:38:32.666 --> 00:38:36.233
Now he's going to try it with
his corporate account which has

00:38:36.233 --> 00:38:42.599
been authorized and he gets the
username, he gets the password,

00:38:42.599 --> 00:38:45.433
but now he will get
prompted to enter his second

00:38:45.433 --> 00:38:46.466
key authentication.

00:38:46.466 --> 00:38:49.000
So if you look on the sides,
I think we're going to project

00:38:49.000 --> 00:38:51.000
that up in a second.

00:38:52.866 --> 00:38:54.766
Well, okay, trust me,
he pushed - there it is.

00:38:54.766 --> 00:38:56.000
He pushed the security key.

00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:58.099
I think you know
what that looks like.

00:38:58.099 --> 00:39:02.033
And now he's taken directly into
the application without having

00:39:02.033 --> 00:39:04.666
to have another
additional login step needed.

00:39:05.666 --> 00:39:08.199
Not only is this easier for the
developer, it's easier for the

00:39:08.199 --> 00:39:10.866
admin, and lord knows it's
easier for the end user rather

00:39:10.866 --> 00:39:13.800
than hassling with a bunch of
VPN software, and it's more

00:39:13.800 --> 00:39:16.466
secure because we're not
trusting anybody who just

00:39:16.466 --> 00:39:18.300
happens to come in
through a VPN tunnel.

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:20.066
So that's IAP.

00:39:21.199 --> 00:39:24.033
The other thing Urs talked
about was data loss prevention.

00:39:24.033 --> 00:39:26.766
Let me show you how Google can
help you secure some of your

00:39:26.766 --> 00:39:28.900
most sensitive data.

00:39:28.900 --> 00:39:32.266
Most companies today have a
policy around data minimization,

00:39:32.266 --> 00:39:35.933
the goal being to minimize the
amount of data that you collect

00:39:35.933 --> 00:39:38.766
and store to only the data
that's actually needed to run

00:39:38.766 --> 00:39:40.366
your business.

00:39:40.366 --> 00:39:44.366
But that's easy to do in words
but hard to do in practice.

00:39:44.366 --> 00:39:46.233
Data has a way of leaking
out all over the place.

00:39:46.233 --> 00:39:48.199
So let's look at a
concrete example.

00:39:48.699 --> 00:39:51.800
We have an enterprise
application that a support agent

00:39:51.800 --> 00:39:54.266
might use to chat
with your end users.

00:39:54.266 --> 00:39:57.099
And in the chat you can see
Neil is a support agent and he's

00:39:57.099 --> 00:40:00.599
asked Alice for some information
to verify her account.

00:40:00.599 --> 00:40:02.466
Well, Alice has way over-shared.

00:40:02.466 --> 00:40:04.699
We just wanted the
last four of her Social.

00:40:04.699 --> 00:40:07.433
She gave us her full Social, her
phone number, and a picture of

00:40:07.433 --> 00:40:10.400
her Social Security card.

00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:15.133
Now obviously we want to store
this chat, right, to see how our

00:40:15.133 --> 00:40:18.333
conversations are going or how
the agents are working, but I

00:40:18.333 --> 00:40:20.133
can't store that in a database.

00:40:20.133 --> 00:40:22.033
I'm going to have half my
company now knowing Alice's

00:40:22.033 --> 00:40:23.766
Social Security Number.

00:40:23.766 --> 00:40:24.633
So how do we do that?

00:40:25.166 --> 00:40:26.233
DLP makes it easy.

00:40:26.233 --> 00:40:29.366
When he ends the chat, we will
use Data Loss Prevention to

00:40:29.366 --> 00:40:33.433
identify and redact
sensitive information.

00:40:33.433 --> 00:40:36.266
So now you'll see
that we've - yes.

00:40:41.300 --> 00:40:45.033
We recognize over 40 different
types of sensitive info types.

00:40:45.033 --> 00:40:47.366
In this case, we've
identified Alice's name.

00:40:47.366 --> 00:40:50.300
If he scrolls down a little bit,
you can see we found her phone

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:53.099
number, her Social Security
Number, we replaced all those

00:40:53.099 --> 00:40:56.666
with red dots, and even we went
into the picture of the Social

00:40:56.666 --> 00:41:00.266
Security card, found the Social
Security Number there and

00:41:00.266 --> 00:41:01.400
redacted it.

00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:03.533
Now this chat can be
used for analysis.

00:41:07.766 --> 00:41:12.866
Now just to prove this is real,
Neil has a webcam over there and

00:41:12.866 --> 00:41:14.666
we're going to
try one more example.

00:41:14.666 --> 00:41:17.166
I was in the green room a while
ago and I found this credit card

00:41:17.166 --> 00:41:19.166
sitting there and
it said Urs Holzle.

00:41:21.566 --> 00:41:22.666
I'm just kidding.

00:41:22.666 --> 00:41:25.966
Neil has a sample credit card
with a valid number but it's not

00:41:25.966 --> 00:41:26.633
Urs' card.

00:41:26.633 --> 00:41:27.699
I trust DLP.

00:41:27.699 --> 00:41:31.199
I don't trust 10,000 of
you with cell phone cameras.

00:41:31.199 --> 00:41:33.533
So he's going to put the credit
card number on it, he's going to

00:41:33.533 --> 00:41:37.766
hit the button, we're going to
use DLP, and it will find and

00:41:37.766 --> 00:41:38.733
redact the credit card.

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:45.133
Now this isn't just dumb OCR.

00:41:45.133 --> 00:41:48.800
You'll notice it didn't block
out the expiration date or the

00:41:48.800 --> 00:41:51.699
name because we told the API
that the only information we

00:41:51.699 --> 00:41:54.000
wanted to eliminate
was credit card numbers.

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:58.699
So you can control your
definition of PII, what matters

00:41:58.699 --> 00:42:00.000
to you.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:00.800
So there you have it.

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:02.099
Identity Aware Proxy.

00:42:02.099 --> 00:42:05.033
We made it super simple to take
a corporate application and make

00:42:05.033 --> 00:42:07.433
it available to our end users
without the hassle of complex

00:42:07.433 --> 00:42:08.533
network configurations.

00:42:08.533 --> 00:42:11.599
And with Data Loss Prevention,
we were able to help it make

00:42:11.599 --> 00:42:14.633
sure that you minimize the
amount of data that you collect

00:42:14.633 --> 00:42:17.099
so that's one less headache you
have in terms of compliance or

00:42:17.099 --> 00:42:19.733
regulatory or really
just running your business.

00:42:19.733 --> 00:42:22.099
And with that, Neil and I will
turn it back over to Urs again.

00:42:22.099 --> 00:42:22.800
Thanks, everybody.

00:42:26.400 --> 00:42:27.300
&gt;&gt; URS HOLZLE: Thank you.

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:27.966
Thank you, Greg.

00:42:29.066 --> 00:42:32.933
So, as you just saw, it's really
easy to use the IAP Proxy to

00:42:32.933 --> 00:42:35.733
control access to
your applications.

00:42:35.733 --> 00:42:38.699
But we're already working on
the next version based on a

00:42:38.699 --> 00:42:41.666
principle that we've been
applying to our own corporate

00:42:41.666 --> 00:42:42.866
users for a while.

00:42:43.966 --> 00:42:49.033
Because we view every access
decision to resource as not

00:42:49.033 --> 00:42:53.500
something that's just about the
user credentials and maybe their

00:42:53.500 --> 00:42:57.166
second factor but really about
something that should be based

00:42:57.166 --> 00:43:00.466
on the context around it; for
example, the state of the user's

00:43:00.466 --> 00:43:03.433
device, their
location, and so on.

00:43:03.433 --> 00:43:06.666
So we call this
Context Aware Security.

00:43:06.666 --> 00:43:10.566
The user's context determines
access, not just the network

00:43:10.566 --> 00:43:13.033
they're on or who they are.

00:43:13.033 --> 00:43:16.966
The context that we have today
with the IAP Proxy is just the

00:43:16.966 --> 00:43:20.400
user identity and the
security key - so the strength

00:43:20.400 --> 00:43:21.699
of authentication.

00:43:21.699 --> 00:43:25.666
But you can expect our future
versions to use a richer and

00:43:25.666 --> 00:43:29.766
richer context over time to
better secure access to your

00:43:29.766 --> 00:43:31.833
Cloud applications
or your G Suite.

00:43:33.099 --> 00:43:36.533
Now I am thrilled to introduce
Brian Stevens to tell you more

00:43:36.533 --> 00:43:40.233
about how customers
adopt GCP. Brian.

00:43:47.733 --> 00:43:50.900
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS: Good morning.

00:43:50.900 --> 00:43:51.900
Thanks, Urs.

00:43:52.366 --> 00:43:56.933
So, public Cloud has absolutely
exited the early adopter stage.

00:43:56.933 --> 00:43:59.833
It's now a shared platform,
available to everyone from

00:43:59.833 --> 00:44:02.699
start-ups to the
world's largest enterprises.

00:44:02.699 --> 00:44:05.466
And it's quickly gone pan
vertical from financial

00:44:05.466 --> 00:44:09.466
services, healthcare,
industrial, government,

00:44:09.466 --> 00:44:12.500
everybody is choosing the Cloud.

00:44:12.500 --> 00:44:15.966
And when they pick GCP,
why do they come to GCP?

00:44:15.966 --> 00:44:17.233
A lot in common.

00:44:17.233 --> 00:44:20.833
They want the
world's best security.

00:44:20.833 --> 00:44:24.833
They want to be on a flywheel
of continued innovation, all

00:44:24.833 --> 00:44:26.833
available through an API.

00:44:28.466 --> 00:44:31.400
They want the best in the
world data and analytics.

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:34.300
And the most important thing,
they want the tools and the

00:44:34.300 --> 00:44:36.333
platforms that
their developers love.

00:44:38.266 --> 00:44:40.900
I've spent a lot of time
working directly with customers.

00:44:40.900 --> 00:44:44.366
And when you actually zoom out
and you try to look at patterns,

00:44:44.366 --> 00:44:46.466
we're actually seeing three
distinct things when they come

00:44:46.466 --> 00:44:48.033
to GCP.

00:44:48.033 --> 00:44:50.833
The first is wholesale
migrations of their existing

00:44:50.833 --> 00:44:54.433
workloads from on-premise to
GCP, which, to be honest, was a

00:44:54.433 --> 00:44:56.699
little bit of a surprise.

00:44:56.699 --> 00:45:00.266
The second is building
Cloud native applications.

00:45:00.266 --> 00:45:03.533
That includes start-ups as well
as some of the world's largest

00:45:03.533 --> 00:45:05.133
enterprises now.

00:45:05.133 --> 00:45:09.133
And the third is just coming to
Cloud to get the richest set of

00:45:09.133 --> 00:45:10.633
data analytics and
machine learning.

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:16.500
So our first Cloud service was
back in 2008, Google App Engine,

00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:19.866
almost nine years ago, and
it was an incredibly powerful

00:45:19.866 --> 00:45:24.033
platform as a service, possibly
too advanced for its time.

00:45:24.033 --> 00:45:26.900
We're going to talk about
that a little bit more soon.

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:30.833
But most enterprises either
can't or don't want to rewrite

00:45:30.833 --> 00:45:33.466
their application
architecture just to move to

00:45:33.466 --> 00:45:35.800
the public Cloud.

00:45:35.800 --> 00:45:38.900
And what's amazing is that
even without that rewrite, the

00:45:38.900 --> 00:45:44.000
drivers for moving to GCP are
still incredibly compelling.

00:45:44.000 --> 00:45:47.599
They get the amazing security
that you just saw, they get to

00:45:47.599 --> 00:45:52.099
reduce their capex, they get
incredible reliability, Google's

00:45:52.099 --> 00:45:56.666
pretty good about reliability,
performance, they get it for

00:45:56.666 --> 00:45:59.166
free, look what you just
saw with Skylake and what's

00:45:59.166 --> 00:46:01.066
happening in the
network, and they get this

00:46:01.066 --> 00:46:02.400
improved efficiency.

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:09.500
So I actually kind of loathe, to
be honest, industry conferences,

00:46:09.500 --> 00:46:12.466
but there's this one CIO
conference that I go to every

00:46:12.466 --> 00:46:15.000
year, and it's put on by Excel,
and it's a small, little,

00:46:15.000 --> 00:46:17.266
intimate venue
with about ten CIOs.

00:46:17.266 --> 00:46:21.099
And two years ago the top things
when we went around the room

00:46:21.099 --> 00:46:23.599
that they cared about was
security, what kept them awake

00:46:23.599 --> 00:46:26.166
at night, and mobile handsets.

00:46:26.166 --> 00:46:28.066
The two are really related.

00:46:28.066 --> 00:46:31.199
A month ago when we went back
again, same group of CIOs, they

00:46:31.199 --> 00:46:34.366
went around the table and the
top two things that were keeping

00:46:34.366 --> 00:46:38.699
them up, security still, and
then shutting down data centers.

00:46:38.699 --> 00:46:41.666
Every one of them was shrinking
their data center footprint.

00:46:42.599 --> 00:46:44.966
I felt a little weird because
- and then it was my turn and I

00:46:44.966 --> 00:46:47.333
was talking about
we're doing the opposite.

00:46:47.333 --> 00:46:49.099
Like look at what
Urs just showed you.

00:46:49.099 --> 00:46:51.800
So it's kind of clear where
these workloads are going.

00:46:53.800 --> 00:46:56.633
So, ideally, you want to be able
to shift the workloads with as

00:46:56.633 --> 00:46:59.666
little change as possible
because you don't want it to be

00:46:59.666 --> 00:47:03.400
a lot of work just to move to
Cloud, and the more change you

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:06.000
introduce, there's more risk.

00:47:06.000 --> 00:47:08.066
But that's just
the first chapter.

00:47:08.666 --> 00:47:12.666
Once you get to GCP, often what
happens is chapter two, and

00:47:12.666 --> 00:47:15.266
that's when they actually
look at how do we re-factor?

00:47:15.266 --> 00:47:17.533
How do we use things
like a managed database?

00:47:17.533 --> 00:47:20.466
How do we use
something like Spanner?

00:47:20.466 --> 00:47:24.466
So our call to action at Google
is how do we make that as simple

00:47:24.466 --> 00:47:27.366
and easy as possible for them,
and that includes technology,

00:47:27.366 --> 00:47:28.599
processes, and people.

00:47:30.466 --> 00:47:33.066
One thing that we did is we
recently added right from the

00:47:33.066 --> 00:47:36.133
Cloud console the ability to
migrate a virtual machine

00:47:36.133 --> 00:47:37.633
to GCP.

00:47:37.633 --> 00:47:41.233
This is more than
transcoding an offline image.

00:47:41.233 --> 00:47:46.099
It's actually the live migration
of a running server to GCP.

00:47:46.099 --> 00:47:48.633
What I love about it is that
it's hypervisor agnostic, so it

00:47:48.633 --> 00:47:53.166
works on bare metal, it works
on Hyper-V, ESX, KVM, even from

00:47:53.166 --> 00:47:53.833
another Cloud.

00:47:55.800 --> 00:47:58.866
Also, we've been making big
investments in Windows because

00:47:58.866 --> 00:48:02.699
we want to make GCP as great
for Windows developers as it has

00:48:02.699 --> 00:48:06.500
been for Linux and
Open Source developers.

00:48:06.500 --> 00:48:09.699
Our goal wasn't just to be
an okay Windows platform.

00:48:09.699 --> 00:48:12.866
We want to be a great Windows
platform, perhaps the best

00:48:12.866 --> 00:48:14.366
Windows platform.

00:48:14.366 --> 00:48:18.033
So we already have support
and pre-built images for many

00:48:18.033 --> 00:48:21.400
flavors, a Windows server,
as well as SQL server.

00:48:21.400 --> 00:48:24.199
And we also support active
directory running in the Cloud,

00:48:24.199 --> 00:48:26.833
and you can integrate
that with your on-premise

00:48:26.833 --> 00:48:27.633
domain controller.

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:33.533
But it's really important for us
that for developers and Windows

00:48:33.533 --> 00:48:36.333
developers that we meet
them exactly where they are.

00:48:36.333 --> 00:48:38.800
We don't want them to have to
change how they do things just

00:48:38.800 --> 00:48:40.833
to be able to take
advantage of Cloud.

00:48:40.833 --> 00:48:43.766
That's why we did the Visual
Studio integration that we've

00:48:43.766 --> 00:48:47.066
done with GCP so that you can
actually deploy dot net apps

00:48:47.066 --> 00:48:49.300
from Visual Studio and then
just manage all of your

00:48:49.300 --> 00:48:51.333
Cloud resources.

00:48:51.333 --> 00:48:54.766
And we've also integrated
with hundreds of cmdlets for

00:48:54.766 --> 00:48:57.466
PowerShell right
into our Cloud SDK.

00:48:57.466 --> 00:49:01.133
And so now they can be very
comfortable managing all of

00:49:01.133 --> 00:49:02.833
their Windows-based
Cloud projects.

00:49:04.466 --> 00:49:07.866
So today we're announcing the
general availability of SQL

00:49:07.866 --> 00:49:10.699
Server Enterprise and that
actually includes support for a

00:49:10.699 --> 00:49:13.866
high availability as
well as clustering.

00:49:13.866 --> 00:49:17.500
Also, a beta dot net and that
will be available in both App

00:49:17.500 --> 00:49:19.866
Engine and Container Engine.

00:49:19.866 --> 00:49:22.066
And to help people on this
journey, we're announcing a new

00:49:22.066 --> 00:49:23.500
Windows partner program.

00:49:23.500 --> 00:49:26.766
And so we've partnered up with
a number of top specialists that

00:49:26.766 --> 00:49:30.733
actually have great
Windows expertise as well as

00:49:30.733 --> 00:49:31.800
GCP expertise.

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:34.533
So they can help customers on
their journey to move Windows

00:49:34.533 --> 00:49:36.033
environments to GCP.

00:49:37.466 --> 00:49:43.566
So now Linux or Windows, my SQL
Oracle or SQL server, and as of

00:49:43.566 --> 00:49:48.166
today also a beta of Managed
Postgres, developers can really

00:49:48.166 --> 00:49:51.233
easily migrate to GCP with
minimal refactoring of their

00:49:51.233 --> 00:49:53.433
application stacks.

00:49:53.433 --> 00:49:56.500
So what's the best test
for moving to the Cloud with

00:49:56.500 --> 00:49:57.866
minimal friction?

00:49:57.866 --> 00:50:01.733
Probably to be able to
do that with zero downtime.

00:50:01.733 --> 00:50:05.500
It seems pretty impossible,
but Evernote actually did it

00:50:05.500 --> 00:50:06.566
last year.

00:50:06.566 --> 00:50:09.833
They moved their entire software
infrastructure from their

00:50:09.833 --> 00:50:16.833
on-premise data centers to GCP -
200 million customers depend on

00:50:16.833 --> 00:50:21.866
Evernote every day - and they
did this migration in 89 days

00:50:21.866 --> 00:50:24.033
with zero downtime.

00:50:24.033 --> 00:50:27.466
And so Lush, a British cosmetics
company, they began their

00:50:27.466 --> 00:50:28.966
migration to GCP.

00:50:28.966 --> 00:50:33.566
They started last year in
September and it was critical

00:50:33.566 --> 00:50:36.733
that they finish in time for the
holiday season and they did it.

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:42.400
And so here, I would like to
introduce Jack Constantine from

00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:44.033
Lush to tell us
about their journey.

00:50:55.933 --> 00:50:56.699
Hi, Jack.

00:50:56.699 --> 00:50:57.833
&gt;&gt; JACK CONSTANTINE: Hi, Brian.

00:50:57.833 --> 00:50:59.466
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS: So 22 days.

00:50:59.466 --> 00:51:01.199
I don't think anybody
is going to believe you.

00:51:01.199 --> 00:51:02.133
It seems impossible.

00:51:02.133 --> 00:51:03.566
How is that even possible?

00:51:03.566 --> 00:51:04.800
&gt;&gt; JACK CONSTANTINE: Yeah.

00:51:04.800 --> 00:51:07.333
So in September we
started discussing doing the

00:51:07.333 --> 00:51:08.433
whole migration.

00:51:08.433 --> 00:51:11.733
The actual migration was from
December the 1st until December

00:51:11.733 --> 00:51:12.900
the 22nd.

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:15.900
So it's not just a little bit
business critical; we're talking

00:51:15.900 --> 00:51:17.866
peak trade time.

00:51:17.866 --> 00:51:20.033
Yeah, so it was a huge deal.

00:51:20.033 --> 00:51:23.333
I mean one of the key things,
I couldn't have done it without

00:51:23.333 --> 00:51:26.400
the in-house engineering team
that we have, some of the guys

00:51:26.400 --> 00:51:27.766
in the audience.

00:51:27.766 --> 00:51:29.666
I wouldn't be up here if it
wasn't for all the hard work

00:51:29.666 --> 00:51:33.033
those guys put in, the hours,
the dedication, the focus.

00:51:33.033 --> 00:51:35.133
I think they deserve
a round of applause.

00:51:40.666 --> 00:51:43.066
We found ourselves in
a bit of a tricky spot.

00:51:43.066 --> 00:51:45.599
We were in a contract that we
weren't really comfortable with.

00:51:45.599 --> 00:51:47.666
We wanted to be able to actually
have a look and see what else we

00:51:47.666 --> 00:51:50.199
could go for.

00:51:50.199 --> 00:51:52.400
The contract ran out on the 22nd
of December, hence the reason

00:51:52.400 --> 00:51:53.733
that we had that hard deadline.

00:51:53.733 --> 00:51:54.833
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS: Lucky us.

00:51:54.833 --> 00:51:56.633
&gt;&gt; JACK CONSTANTINE: Yeah.

00:51:56.633 --> 00:52:02.033
I'm a little bit of a risk-taker
myself, as you can tell.

00:52:02.033 --> 00:52:06.333
Basically there is very little
bureaucracy in Lush, so the

00:52:06.333 --> 00:52:10.166
ability for me to be able to
actually make that decision was

00:52:10.166 --> 00:52:12.966
quite fast and then the
team just powered through.

00:52:12.966 --> 00:52:17.733
It was a really exciting time.

00:52:17.733 --> 00:52:20.666
We were really, obviously,
so pleased with the result.

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:23.400
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS: So were the
like any sort of challenges with

00:52:23.400 --> 00:52:25.866
the migration,
technical inhibitors, process?

00:52:26.599 --> 00:52:28.333
&gt;&gt; JACK CONSTANTINE: Well I
think, like with any migration,

00:52:28.333 --> 00:52:29.866
there are always
technical challenges.

00:52:29.866 --> 00:52:32.633
You've got to worry about
getting your data from one place

00:52:32.633 --> 00:52:34.566
to the other and the amount of
it and are you going to make

00:52:34.566 --> 00:52:36.366
sure you've got consistency.

00:52:36.366 --> 00:52:40.333
We moved 17 websites from all
over the world with customer

00:52:40.333 --> 00:52:42.500
data, with order
data, product data.

00:52:42.500 --> 00:52:46.233
Obviously you want all of that
to be completely up and stable.

00:52:46.233 --> 00:52:50.066
But I think one of the
main things really from my

00:52:50.066 --> 00:52:53.199
perspective was the
kind of commitment to actually

00:52:53.199 --> 00:52:54.300
achieving it.

00:52:54.300 --> 00:52:56.766
I think sometimes people can -
obviously when you're going to

00:52:56.766 --> 00:52:59.166
throw a kind of hard deadline
like that, it can sound a

00:52:59.166 --> 00:53:00.366
bit unachievable.

00:53:00.366 --> 00:53:04.300
But I always like to think it
depends on kind of which reality

00:53:04.300 --> 00:53:06.133
you're looking from.

00:53:06.133 --> 00:53:08.099
I like to think that it's
realistic; you may think it's

00:53:08.099 --> 00:53:09.066
not realistic. Right?

00:53:10.033 --> 00:53:12.033
I think it's realistic so I
think we should try and do it.

00:53:12.033 --> 00:53:15.933
So keeping that focus on people
actually kind of believing that

00:53:15.933 --> 00:53:18.400
this is a goal we can achieve
in the timeframe and not letting

00:53:18.400 --> 00:53:20.800
people start to put the blockers
up and go, oh, we're going to

00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:22.466
have to delay this, we're
going to have to delay that.

00:53:22.466 --> 00:53:24.966
All of a sudden you watch
everyone - I mean, yeah,

00:53:24.966 --> 00:53:28.366
everyone gets very tense,
but also you achieve a lot.

00:53:28.366 --> 00:53:30.933
You actually get through it and
the things you need to get done

00:53:30.933 --> 00:53:32.933
get done.

00:53:32.933 --> 00:53:34.599
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS: Anything that
could have made it a little

00:53:34.599 --> 00:53:35.900
bit easier?

00:53:35.900 --> 00:53:37.033
&gt;&gt; JACK CONSTANTINE:
Oh, definitely.

00:53:37.033 --> 00:53:41.066
I think the awkwardness of our
previous supplier and the fact

00:53:41.066 --> 00:53:44.000
that it was a very closed
environment made it very

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:47.099
difficult for us to get
visibility of everything we

00:53:47.099 --> 00:53:48.566
wanted to be able to move over.

00:53:48.566 --> 00:53:49.266
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS:
Open wins again.

00:53:49.266 --> 00:53:50.300
&gt;&gt; JACK CONSTANTINE: Exactly.

00:53:50.300 --> 00:53:54.066
Openness is something that we
absolutely cherish in Lush.

00:53:54.066 --> 00:53:57.266
Obviously you guys do at Google,
which has been great for us.

00:53:57.266 --> 00:54:00.133
And the other thing that we had,
we had this great - one of my

00:54:00.133 --> 00:54:01.766
colleagues had this great
conversation with a Google

00:54:01.766 --> 00:54:04.166
partner on the phone and it was
about a week before and they

00:54:04.166 --> 00:54:06.400
were obviously getting a bit
scared, oh, is this going to

00:54:06.400 --> 00:54:08.633
happen, and they
were on the phone.

00:54:08.633 --> 00:54:10.966
The partner said what is Plan B?

00:54:10.966 --> 00:54:14.866
My colleagues said Plan B
is to make Plan A happen.

00:54:14.866 --> 00:54:15.766
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS:
That's a great line.

00:54:15.766 --> 00:54:17.133
&gt;&gt; JACK CONSTANTINE:
That's it. That's it.

00:54:17.966 --> 00:54:18.766
And we made it happen.

00:54:19.966 --> 00:54:22.466
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS: That's cool.

00:54:22.466 --> 00:54:26.533
Urs, the last speaker, was the
one who recruited me to Google.

00:54:26.533 --> 00:54:28.733
I was already sold because I
believed the future is public

00:54:28.733 --> 00:54:31.099
Cloud and just fascinated by it.

00:54:31.099 --> 00:54:33.166
You have to be a technology
company to win this and just

00:54:33.166 --> 00:54:35.433
everything that Google has
been investing in for years

00:54:35.433 --> 00:54:37.166
in technology.

00:54:37.166 --> 00:54:39.400
But the surprise for me when
I actually got here was the

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:45.533
culture, right, the ethics,
the diversity, the focus on

00:54:45.533 --> 00:54:47.900
inclusion, sustainability.

00:54:47.900 --> 00:54:50.500
And in our chat yesterday, it
sounds like there are a few

00:54:50.500 --> 00:54:53.366
similarities in your culture
that you and your parents have.

00:54:53.566 --> 00:54:54.699
&gt;&gt; JACK CONSTANTINE: Yeah. Yeah.

00:54:54.699 --> 00:54:58.333
So my parents founded Lush over
20 years ago and there are a lot

00:54:58.333 --> 00:55:01.400
of ethical values that
we've built throughout the

00:55:01.400 --> 00:55:04.733
organization, and we pride
that in kind of everyone we go

00:55:04.733 --> 00:55:07.533
through, supply chain when we're
buying ingredients, and fair

00:55:07.533 --> 00:55:10.366
trade, and looking at the best
quality ingredients, we look at

00:55:10.366 --> 00:55:12.533
that with our packaging.

00:55:12.533 --> 00:55:16.566
I represent the more digital
side of the business, obviously.

00:55:16.566 --> 00:55:20.699
It's a very interesting time and
Lush is looking at its digital

00:55:20.699 --> 00:55:24.733
future and understanding where
we go and how we navigate

00:55:24.733 --> 00:55:25.733
through the landscape.

00:55:25.733 --> 00:55:28.033
I think there are a lot of
similarities with Google in

00:55:28.033 --> 00:55:33.300
terms of the openness versus
closed, the cultural elements.

00:55:33.300 --> 00:55:36.199
It's been great to be able to
- one of the reasons we were so

00:55:36.199 --> 00:55:40.266
eager to do the migration in
that time period was because it

00:55:40.266 --> 00:55:42.966
felt like by moving over to the
Google Cloud platform we would

00:55:42.966 --> 00:55:45.766
be aligning with our ethical
values on a much higher level.

00:55:45.766 --> 00:55:50.366
Things like the renewable energy
and the open mentality, all of

00:55:50.366 --> 00:55:53.366
those things, we're looking
at the moment around digital

00:55:53.366 --> 00:55:54.366
ethics policy.

00:55:54.866 --> 00:55:58.333
Actually, just before we did the
migration in November, we did a

00:55:58.333 --> 00:56:02.566
global campaign to support
keeping the Internet on,

00:56:02.566 --> 00:56:05.666
especially in countries where
the government may shut the

00:56:05.666 --> 00:56:09.300
Internet down because they don't
want to encourage communication.

00:56:09.300 --> 00:56:11.699
Obviously kind of the reverse
of what we wanted to happen with

00:56:11.699 --> 00:56:12.733
our migration.

00:56:12.733 --> 00:56:15.833
Thankfully we also kept the
Internet on when we migrated

00:56:15.833 --> 00:56:17.233
our websites.

00:56:17.233 --> 00:56:21.400
But, yeah, the synergy between
Google and Lush is great.

00:56:21.400 --> 00:56:24.599
I'm really excited about even
the things you've been showing

00:56:24.599 --> 00:56:27.566
today and being able to
work together much more on

00:56:27.566 --> 00:56:30.533
prototyping new ideas,
having that flexibility.

00:56:32.066 --> 00:56:34.466
I spoke to Urs earlier and he
was saying about that whole kind

00:56:34.466 --> 00:56:36.099
of engineer-to-engineer dynamic.

00:56:36.099 --> 00:56:39.333
And my team straight away,
they were absolutely buzzing.

00:56:39.333 --> 00:56:42.466
We've only been working with you
guys for the last 3-4 months but

00:56:42.466 --> 00:56:44.300
the energy is huge.

00:56:44.300 --> 00:56:45.699
It's great.

00:56:45.699 --> 00:56:47.133
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS: Well, my wife
and daughter have always been

00:56:47.133 --> 00:56:49.433
big fans, but I'll say
I'm a convert now as well.

00:56:49.433 --> 00:56:50.166
Thanks, Jack.

00:56:50.166 --> 00:56:50.699
&gt;&gt; JACK CONSTANTINE:
Thanks very much.

00:56:57.166 --> 00:56:58.500
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS:
That's a good story.

00:56:58.500 --> 00:57:02.000
So Evernote and Lush are great
examples of a complete migration

00:57:02.000 --> 00:57:05.633
to GCP from either another
Cloud or on-premise.

00:57:05.633 --> 00:57:10.333
But for the largest enterprises,
the move to Cloud isn't a point

00:57:10.333 --> 00:57:11.166
in time.

00:57:11.166 --> 00:57:13.433
It's going to be a
perpetual state to run in a

00:57:13.433 --> 00:57:14.800
hybrid environment.

00:57:14.800 --> 00:57:17.466
And what we don't want is we
don't want this steel curtain

00:57:17.466 --> 00:57:19.500
between public
Cloud and private.

00:57:19.500 --> 00:57:23.166
It should really feel like this
data center extension, albeit

00:57:23.166 --> 00:57:25.366
this really amazing
data center extension.

00:57:26.199 --> 00:57:31.533
So cornerstone to that is
Virtual Private Cloud, VPC.

00:57:31.533 --> 00:57:34.800
That really allows enterprises
to build these really nice

00:57:34.800 --> 00:57:36.466
integrated hybrid environments.

00:57:36.466 --> 00:57:39.366
It gives them a completely
private virtual network running

00:57:39.366 --> 00:57:41.033
inside of GCP.

00:57:41.033 --> 00:57:42.933
It used to be that everything
running on Cloud had public

00:57:42.933 --> 00:57:44.066
IP addresses.

00:57:44.066 --> 00:57:47.533
Now with VPCs you can have a
completely private environment,

00:57:47.533 --> 00:57:51.366
private IP addresses,
private DNS, and full control.

00:57:51.966 --> 00:57:55.966
And you want to be able to
also control what applications

00:57:55.966 --> 00:58:01.066
outside of your VPC can actually
have ingressed in as well as to

00:58:01.066 --> 00:58:03.533
make sure you have full control
of anything running inside your

00:58:03.533 --> 00:58:06.966
VPC that you allow to
talk to the outside world.

00:58:06.966 --> 00:58:10.199
And on top of all that, you
always want full auditability

00:58:10.199 --> 00:58:13.133
and full telemetry so that you
really see everything that's

00:58:13.133 --> 00:58:15.466
going on, even inside of
these managed services.

00:58:15.466 --> 00:58:16.633
They should not be opaque.

00:58:19.133 --> 00:58:22.500
Also, you don't want application
in data silos forced on you just

00:58:22.500 --> 00:58:24.599
because of this
move to public Cloud.

00:58:24.599 --> 00:58:28.699
That was really one of several
drivers behind the acquisition

00:58:28.699 --> 00:58:29.766
of Apigee.

00:58:29.766 --> 00:58:34.466
So with Apigee, you can actually
put really elegant APIs in front

00:58:34.466 --> 00:58:39.366
of technology so you turn them
into consumable services and

00:58:39.366 --> 00:58:43.500
that allows you to integrate
applications in these services

00:58:43.500 --> 00:58:46.099
whether they're built on
Cloud and integrate them into

00:58:46.099 --> 00:58:49.533
on-premise into your enterprise
or on-premise and integrate them

00:58:49.533 --> 00:58:52.300
into your Cloud applications.

00:58:52.300 --> 00:58:55.966
So soon Chet Kapoor, the VP of
Apigee, is going to be on stage

00:58:55.966 --> 00:58:59.866
and he's going to go into far
more detail on how they're

00:58:59.866 --> 00:59:01.733
helping in building
connected business platforms.

00:59:03.666 --> 00:59:05.766
So Docker has been
this amazing thing.

00:59:05.766 --> 00:59:09.699
It's been this amazing gift to
the industry what Docker has

00:59:09.699 --> 00:59:13.633
created because it's really the
first time that you've actually

00:59:13.633 --> 00:59:16.766
been able to build these
consistent application stacks

00:59:16.766 --> 00:59:19.300
that run across hypervisors,
different operating systems, and

00:59:19.300 --> 00:59:20.366
different Cloud.

00:59:20.366 --> 00:59:24.433
It's not perfect in terms of
compatibility but it's really

00:59:24.433 --> 00:59:26.066
setting us free to do
some amazing things.

00:59:26.066 --> 00:59:30.900
And it's because of Docker that
Kubernetes is so successful.

00:59:31.599 --> 00:59:35.133
Because now with Kubernetes, you
need a control plane, you need

00:59:35.133 --> 00:59:38.033
to be able to manage
and orchestrate these

00:59:38.033 --> 00:59:41.833
container-based environments
and that's what Kubernetes does.

00:59:41.833 --> 00:59:47.599
So it's really quickly become
that de facto operational model.

00:59:47.599 --> 00:59:50.400
And because it's open source,
we're seeing customers,

00:59:50.400 --> 00:59:53.199
enterprises run Kubernetes
on-premise and then they're

00:59:53.199 --> 00:59:56.033
running our managed service
for Kubernetes, the container

00:59:56.033 --> 00:59:57.066
engine, on Cloud.

00:59:57.066 --> 01:00:00.033
It gives them this single
operational model that's

01:00:00.033 --> 01:00:03.599
entirely consistent across
hybrid environments or they can

01:00:03.599 --> 01:00:06.199
integrate them all together and
run a single control plane on

01:00:06.199 --> 01:00:08.466
GCP to even manage
the on-premise world.

01:00:11.300 --> 01:00:14.166
Serverless is a
really important concept.

01:00:14.166 --> 01:00:16.366
Developers shouldn't
need to think about

01:00:16.366 --> 01:00:18.433
managing infrastructure.

01:00:18.433 --> 01:00:21.199
Servers should be provisioned
automatically and just sized to

01:00:21.199 --> 01:00:22.266
the workload.

01:00:22.266 --> 01:00:24.633
And engineered right, it's more
reliable, it's easier, it's

01:00:24.633 --> 01:00:25.699
more efficient.

01:00:25.699 --> 01:00:28.966
The spoiler alert is the
servers are still there.

01:00:28.966 --> 01:00:30.733
When you need them,
they become plentiful.

01:00:30.733 --> 01:00:33.500
And when you don't need
them, they just go away.

01:00:33.500 --> 01:00:35.033
It's really been
a design approach.

01:00:35.033 --> 01:00:36.666
Serverless is not new to Google.

01:00:36.666 --> 01:00:38.566
It's been a design
approach across many of our

01:00:38.566 --> 01:00:39.633
major services.

01:00:39.633 --> 01:00:42.300
On the compute side, App
Engine and Container Engine

01:00:42.300 --> 01:00:43.300
are serverless.

01:00:43.300 --> 01:00:45.900
On the database side, Datastore
and Spanner you don't see

01:00:45.900 --> 01:00:49.099
infrastructure, and
also with BigQuery.

01:00:49.099 --> 01:00:52.466
Each service consumes no servers
when there is no load and they

01:00:52.466 --> 01:00:55.966
all scale out horizontally for
when you need more horsepower.

01:00:58.199 --> 01:01:01.300
And today we're announcing the
beta of our newest serverless

01:01:01.300 --> 01:01:03.033
offering - Cloud Functions.

01:01:08.666 --> 01:01:11.333
So functions are simply
fragments of code and they

01:01:11.333 --> 01:01:12.166
get applause.

01:01:12.166 --> 01:01:13.233
That's great.

01:01:13.233 --> 01:01:15.500
But what developers do with
these fragments of code is they

01:01:15.500 --> 01:01:19.333
connect services together and
they plug them into a growing

01:01:19.333 --> 01:01:24.266
corpus of events across GCP and
that's how they tether it in.

01:01:24.266 --> 01:01:26.566
But what it does is powerful.

01:01:26.566 --> 01:01:30.800
It lets you take this generic
Cloud that's good for everybody

01:01:30.800 --> 01:01:33.699
and you can personalize it in a
way that is meaningful to you.

01:01:34.300 --> 01:01:37.166
During the alpha, we saw people
do some pretty amazing things.

01:01:37.166 --> 01:01:42.500
One example that I love was one
company actually looked at event

01:01:42.500 --> 01:01:45.433
logs and then they keyed off
certain events in the event logs

01:01:45.433 --> 01:01:47.833
and they actually automatically filed bugs in JIRA.

01:01:47.833 --> 01:01:48.933
I think that was pretty cool.

01:01:48.933 --> 01:01:51.500
And then we also see
people doing PII scanning.

01:01:51.500 --> 01:01:54.266
They plug in for the PII they
care about, and any time an

01:01:54.266 --> 01:01:57.966
object comes into GCS, they
can actually scan for it.

01:01:57.966 --> 01:02:02.699
So this ability to be able to
extend GCP in a way both for

01:02:02.699 --> 01:02:04.900
developers as well
as system administrators are

01:02:04.900 --> 01:02:06.000
virtually endless.

01:02:06.000 --> 01:02:12.800
So I mentioned earlier that back
in 2008 Google App Engine was

01:02:12.800 --> 01:02:15.500
really this pioneering
serverless and it was ahead of

01:02:15.500 --> 01:02:16.599
its time.

01:02:16.599 --> 01:02:20.566
The core promise to developers
still remains the same - bring

01:02:20.566 --> 01:02:23.166
your code and Google will
handle everything else.

01:02:25.366 --> 01:02:29.533
It empowers applications to
scale from one request per day

01:02:29.533 --> 01:02:31.466
up to millions of
requests per second.

01:02:31.466 --> 01:02:34.633
And so when you actually
liberate developers from

01:02:34.633 --> 01:02:37.500
managing and patching servers,
dealing with scale, dealing with

01:02:37.500 --> 01:02:41.566
load balancers, version
rollouts, managing databases,

01:02:41.566 --> 01:02:43.066
they can create great things.

01:02:43.066 --> 01:02:46.133
That's what's been
happening at Snap, Home Depot,

01:02:46.133 --> 01:02:47.099
and Philips Lighting.

01:02:47.099 --> 01:02:49.966
And internally to Google,
App Engine has been around a

01:02:49.966 --> 01:02:51.033
long time.

01:02:51.033 --> 01:02:53.766
So our corporate IT, we have
thousands of App Engine-based

01:02:53.766 --> 01:02:57.766
apps in production that Googlers
depend on each and every day.

01:02:59.566 --> 01:03:03.066
So beginning today, we're
delivering a promise to Google

01:03:03.066 --> 01:03:07.066
App Engine but we're making it
available to entirely expanded

01:03:07.066 --> 01:03:08.400
developer communities.

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:12.366
It's a focus on more
openness, developer choice, and

01:03:12.366 --> 01:03:13.966
expanded portability.

01:03:13.966 --> 01:03:17.433
So out of the box, we now
support seven popular languages

01:03:17.433 --> 01:03:23.166
- Java, Ruby, Go, PHP, Python,
C#, and Node - or you can now,

01:03:23.166 --> 01:03:26.133
for the first time, bring your
own runtime, bring your own

01:03:26.133 --> 01:03:28.800
framework to App Engine
Flexible Environment.

01:03:28.800 --> 01:03:31.666
As long as it runs in the
Docker Container, it now runs

01:03:31.666 --> 01:03:32.599
on App Engine.

01:03:36.666 --> 01:03:37.699
Thank you.

01:03:40.233 --> 01:03:42.333
So I can't think of where
serverless would matter more but

01:03:42.333 --> 01:03:44.033
to mobile developers.

01:03:44.033 --> 01:03:46.666
Do you know a mobile
developer that wants to manage

01:03:46.666 --> 01:03:47.800
operating systems?

01:03:47.800 --> 01:03:51.266
What they want to focus on is
building a great user experience

01:03:51.266 --> 01:03:52.699
for their users.

01:03:52.699 --> 01:03:56.266
So we've been working really
closely with the Firebase team,

01:03:56.266 --> 01:03:58.300
which is Google's mobile
application development

01:03:58.300 --> 01:04:02.066
platform, which supports
iOS, Android, and Web.

01:04:02.066 --> 01:04:05.099
They actually jut surpassed
a milestone last month.

01:04:05.099 --> 01:04:10.233
In the last 11 months, they now
have one million active projects

01:04:10.233 --> 01:04:12.366
on Firebase.

01:04:12.366 --> 01:04:14.366
So, really amazing momentum.

01:04:14.366 --> 01:04:17.333
Today we're actually making
Firebase even more powerful

01:04:17.333 --> 01:04:20.566
because we're integrating it
closer so that you can access

01:04:20.566 --> 01:04:22.599
GCP resources
right from Firebase.

01:04:24.466 --> 01:04:27.599
The first thing we did is we
integrated Cloud storage into

01:04:27.599 --> 01:04:32.000
the Firebase SDKs, so now
you can access any GCS bucket

01:04:32.000 --> 01:04:34.933
anywhere in the
world right from Firebase.

01:04:34.933 --> 01:04:39.033
So you get this direct to mobile
upload and download for every

01:04:39.033 --> 01:04:41.233
Cloud storage user
from a mobile platform.

01:04:43.333 --> 01:04:46.366
One thing that people love about
Firebase is it has this amazing

01:04:46.366 --> 01:04:50.500
built-in analytics capability
and that's what developers use

01:04:50.500 --> 01:04:53.933
to understand their users.

01:04:53.933 --> 01:04:56.400
Now we've actually integrated
that so you can actually take

01:04:56.400 --> 01:05:00.300
the analytics data in Firebase
and then drive it into BigQuery

01:05:00.300 --> 01:05:03.533
and that allows even
more analysis and really

01:05:03.533 --> 01:05:05.166
understanding your
users in realtime.

01:05:07.566 --> 01:05:10.166
And we've also integrated the
Cloud Functions that we just

01:05:10.166 --> 01:05:12.900
talked about with Firebase,
and so that allows Firebase

01:05:12.900 --> 01:05:17.066
developers to extend their
backend logic in crazy ways.

01:05:17.066 --> 01:05:19.566
They can send push
notifications, transform data to

01:05:19.566 --> 01:05:23.699
machine learning, define custom
business logic, all with just a

01:05:23.699 --> 01:05:24.900
few lines of code.

01:05:24.900 --> 01:05:27.933
In fact, the integration with
Cloud Functions has been the

01:05:27.933 --> 01:05:30.966
number one feature request of
Firebase developers over the

01:05:30.966 --> 01:05:32.966
last year.

01:05:33.633 --> 01:05:36.466
And then finally, we love
lawyers almost as much as we

01:05:36.466 --> 01:05:40.199
love developers, so we're
actually working to extend GCP's

01:05:40.199 --> 01:05:43.766
terms of service to cover
many of the Firebase products.

01:05:43.766 --> 01:05:46.766
So we're not there yet but soon
you'll be able to bundle much of

01:05:46.766 --> 01:05:49.433
Firebase into essentially the
same contract that you use

01:05:49.433 --> 01:05:50.099
for GCP.

01:05:52.533 --> 01:05:54.333
So we'll be making a lot
more integrations over the

01:05:54.333 --> 01:05:55.666
coming months.

01:05:55.666 --> 01:05:59.566
Greg is coming back now and he's
going to show you how we use GCP

01:05:59.566 --> 01:06:02.900
and Firebase together
to really modernize corporate

01:06:02.900 --> 01:06:04.766
IT apps. Greg.

01:06:10.066 --> 01:06:12.533
&gt;&gt; GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Thanks, Brian.

01:06:12.533 --> 01:06:15.366
Brian told you we're going to
take an example of an existing

01:06:15.366 --> 01:06:18.366
on-premise application and
we're going to modernize it with

01:06:18.366 --> 01:06:19.433
Google Cloud.

01:06:19.433 --> 01:06:22.033
Now in this case, the app that
Chris and I are going to work

01:06:22.033 --> 01:06:24.733
with is an old
ASP.net application.

01:06:29.400 --> 01:06:31.166
Well that's not an
ASP.net application.

01:06:37.633 --> 01:06:39.133
That's an ASP.net application.

01:06:39.699 --> 01:06:42.566
This is an application used
by an insurance company.

01:06:42.566 --> 01:06:44.500
So the adjusters go out in the
field, they take their digital

01:06:44.500 --> 01:06:47.233
camera, they take pictures of
the accidents, they put it in

01:06:47.233 --> 01:06:50.199
their SD cards, and then
they upload the images into

01:06:50.199 --> 01:06:52.033
the application.

01:06:52.033 --> 01:06:55.599
It's also got an API on it so
that you can manage all the

01:06:55.599 --> 01:06:58.199
images that are
associated with a given claim.

01:06:58.199 --> 01:07:00.733
Now we're going to start
bringing this application out of

01:07:00.733 --> 01:07:03.266
the '90s and
into the modern era.

01:07:03.266 --> 01:07:05.900
We're going to start by giving
our agents a good mobile

01:07:05.900 --> 01:07:09.033
application so they don't
have to lug a laptop around.

01:07:09.033 --> 01:07:11.366
Firebase makes that super easy.

01:07:11.366 --> 01:07:14.366
You can build a mobile
application without having to be

01:07:14.366 --> 01:07:15.566
a backend expert.

01:07:16.400 --> 01:07:19.766
In this case, what Chris is
showing you is the line of code

01:07:19.766 --> 01:07:23.500
that integrates
Firebase with Cloud Storage.

01:07:23.500 --> 01:07:26.900
So this line of code allows the
camera to take a picture, that

01:07:26.900 --> 01:07:30.000
picture then automatically gets
uploaded into a Google Cloud

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:31.233
Storage bucket.

01:07:31.233 --> 01:07:33.233
So to show how this is going to
happen, Chris is going to take a

01:07:33.233 --> 01:07:37.900
quick photo
with the phone. Yep.

01:07:39.533 --> 01:07:43.933
&gt;&gt; CHRIS: All right,
everyone, say cheese.

01:07:43.933 --> 01:07:46.333
I love having audiences do that.

01:07:46.333 --> 01:07:48.533
&gt;&gt; GREG DEMICHILLIE: So that
photo now is being uploaded and

01:07:48.533 --> 01:07:51.199
it's being stored into a
Google Cloud Storage bucket.

01:07:51.199 --> 01:07:53.800
Now to show that it really is
in a GCS bucket, we're going to

01:07:53.800 --> 01:07:55.566
switch over and we're going
to list the contents of

01:07:55.566 --> 01:07:56.866
this bucket.

01:07:56.866 --> 01:07:59.400
Now the Windows users among you
are going to notice that he's

01:07:59.400 --> 01:08:01.000
using PowerShell.

01:08:01.000 --> 01:08:03.333
That's because he's using
Cloud tools for PowerShell.

01:08:03.333 --> 01:08:06.900
It gives Windows developers a
first class experience using

01:08:06.900 --> 01:08:09.666
Google Cloud platform with
the services that they love.

01:08:09.666 --> 01:08:11.133
And sure enough, there
is the picture that was just

01:08:11.133 --> 01:08:12.766
uploaded today.

01:08:12.766 --> 01:08:17.133
So now we've got a claim picture
in GCS, how do we get it to this

01:08:17.133 --> 01:08:19.333
old legacy application?

01:08:19.333 --> 01:08:22.466
Apigee and Cloud
Functions make that simple.

01:08:22.466 --> 01:08:26.500
The good news is our legacy
app has an API, as I mentioned.

01:08:26.500 --> 01:08:29.166
The bad news is the app was
written over a decade ago so

01:08:29.166 --> 01:08:30.833
it's a SOAP API.

01:08:30.833 --> 01:08:34.399
So it's not really very friendly
to a modern restful developer

01:08:34.399 --> 01:08:36.033
like Chris.

01:08:36.033 --> 01:08:38.600
Apigee, however,
is an Enterprise API

01:08:38.600 --> 01:08:39.699
Management platform.

01:08:39.699 --> 01:08:44.033
We're going to start by using
it to create a proxy to convert

01:08:44.033 --> 01:08:47.566
this legacy
SOAP XML into modern JSON.

01:08:47.566 --> 01:08:49.633
So here is the Apigee console.

01:08:49.633 --> 01:08:52.433
We've connected it to
the WSDL of the service.

01:08:52.433 --> 01:08:54.500
I can't believe I'm
saying that in 2017.

01:08:54.500 --> 01:08:59.233
And on the left side, you see
the SOAP XML, and on the right

01:08:59.233 --> 01:09:02.566
side, Apigee has automatically
created a much friendlier,

01:09:02.566 --> 01:09:06.000
easier for the developer
to use, JSON version of it.

01:09:06.800 --> 01:09:08.699
Now Apigee has a lot
of other capabilities.

01:09:08.699 --> 01:09:12.399
So, in addition to doing that,
Chris has applied a quota so

01:09:12.399 --> 01:09:15.633
that we don't flood this poor
service with millions of mobile

01:09:15.633 --> 01:09:17.866
agents all trying to upload
photos at the same time.

01:09:17.866 --> 01:09:20.966
It automatically
provides throttling.

01:09:20.966 --> 01:09:24.333
We've also put authentication in
so that only our agents and only

01:09:24.333 --> 01:09:27.833
those mobile applications
can upload the data.

01:09:27.833 --> 01:09:31.266
Now Apigee has got tons
more capabilities; I am only

01:09:31.266 --> 01:09:32.500
scratching the surface.

01:09:32.500 --> 01:09:34.233
But for our purposes,
that's good enough for

01:09:34.233 --> 01:09:35.000
this application.

01:09:35.866 --> 01:09:38.733
So we've got images in a GCS
bucket, we got our application

01:09:38.733 --> 01:09:42.033
that's now got a nice modern
API on it, how do we connect

01:09:42.033 --> 01:09:43.133
the two?

01:09:43.133 --> 01:09:47.500
Well I could deploy a VM,
but that's overkill, right?

01:09:47.500 --> 01:09:50.633
All I want to do is copy a
little file from GCS to an API.

01:09:50.633 --> 01:09:53.533
Why should I pick an operating
system and have to patch the OS

01:09:53.533 --> 01:09:55.833
and deploy a big,
heavy VM image?

01:09:55.833 --> 01:09:58.233
Cloud Functions allows me
to just write a snippet

01:09:58.233 --> 01:09:59.600
of Javascript.

01:09:59.600 --> 01:10:02.600
This Javascript is
listening to that GCS bucket.

01:10:02.600 --> 01:10:06.266
So any time a file gets added,
this snippet gets invoked.

01:10:06.266 --> 01:10:09.433
And if he goes down, you'll see
he is using the post method,

01:10:09.433 --> 01:10:13.133
that nice, restful
interface onto our web service.

01:10:13.133 --> 01:10:15.899
So now when Chris takes a
picture, we should see the

01:10:15.899 --> 01:10:16.833
picture go all the way through.

01:10:16.833 --> 01:10:19.500
Now I don't have a car
crash on stage, Chris.

01:10:19.500 --> 01:10:21.699
I think you've got though
some sort of a next best thing

01:10:21.699 --> 01:10:22.766
for us?

01:10:22.766 --> 01:10:26.466
&gt;&gt; CHRIS: Actually, what I am
prepared to do, Greg, is I've

01:10:26.466 --> 01:10:31.000
got some scale model vehicle
replicas here that I am going to

01:10:31.000 --> 01:10:34.633
use to simulate a high
speed collision scenario.

01:10:34.633 --> 01:10:35.933
&gt;&gt; GREG DEMICHILLIE: This is
scaled down in a digital

01:10:35.933 --> 01:10:36.733
scale up.

01:10:36.733 --> 01:10:37.466
Yeah, very good.

01:10:37.466 --> 01:10:38.133
&gt;&gt; CHRIS: You ready?

01:10:38.133 --> 01:10:38.766
&gt;&gt; GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Yeah, go for it.

01:10:38.766 --> 01:10:42.633
&gt;&gt; CHRIS: Vroom, crash. Ah!

01:10:47.199 --> 01:10:48.199
&gt;&gt; GREG DEMICHILLIE: Please
tell me you didn't misplace

01:10:48.199 --> 01:10:50.800
the phone.

01:10:50.800 --> 01:10:53.033
So he's going to take the
picture of, oh, the humanity,

01:10:56.500 --> 01:10:59.766
and it gets uploaded, and now
he's going to switch back to our

01:10:59.766 --> 01:11:01.500
application, our corporate app.

01:11:01.500 --> 01:11:03.566
He's going to hit F5 because
it's the '90s and we have

01:11:03.566 --> 01:11:05.433
to refresh.

01:11:05.433 --> 01:11:06.933
And there we go,
there is our application.

01:11:10.333 --> 01:11:13.333
We started with a legacy on-prem
app, we used Firebase to build a

01:11:13.333 --> 01:11:16.666
mobile application that didn't
even require a backend expert,

01:11:16.666 --> 01:11:21.100
we wrapped the old SOAP API in
modern JSON, and Cloud Functions

01:11:21.100 --> 01:11:24.433
was this wonderful glue layer
to connect everything together.

01:11:24.433 --> 01:11:25.733
But one more thing.

01:11:25.733 --> 01:11:28.933
This application started as an
on-prem application and we've

01:11:28.933 --> 01:11:31.399
wrapped it with all this amazing
Cloud stuff, but it's still

01:11:31.399 --> 01:11:33.066
sitting on-prem.

01:11:33.066 --> 01:11:34.233
Let's fix that bug, too.

01:11:34.933 --> 01:11:40.300
With just a couple clicks, Chris
will start the Migration Wizard.

01:11:40.300 --> 01:11:43.066
In about 45 minutes, we will
live migrate this running

01:11:43.066 --> 01:11:45.533
service into Google Cloud
platform where it runs on

01:11:45.533 --> 01:11:47.000
Google's amazing infrastructure,

01:11:47.000 --> 01:11:49.266
network, operations,
and reliability.

01:11:49.266 --> 01:11:51.266
And now we've really taken this
app and brought it into the

01:11:51.266 --> 01:11:52.066
modern era.

01:11:52.066 --> 01:11:54.266
Now I'll turn it
back over to Brian. Thanks.

01:11:59.766 --> 01:12:02.766
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS: Thanks, Greg.

01:12:02.766 --> 01:12:07.566
So, great companies use data
to react faster to the market,

01:12:07.566 --> 01:12:10.266
build novel new products, it
even changed culturally how

01:12:10.266 --> 01:12:11.699
they work.

01:12:11.699 --> 01:12:14.966
Breaking down data silos to see
bigger and better pictures of

01:12:14.966 --> 01:12:18.266
what's going on in realtime
is really super important.

01:12:18.266 --> 01:12:21.866
And so for GCP to enable that,
we deliver an end-to-end managed

01:12:21.866 --> 01:12:22.966
analytics platform.

01:12:22.966 --> 01:12:26.533
It now spins storage, data
warehousing, ingestion, you saw

01:12:26.533 --> 01:12:31.899
PII cleaning, ETL, batch and
streaming modes, visualization,

01:12:31.899 --> 01:12:33.566
and even machine learning.

01:12:33.566 --> 01:12:37.500
It is expanded and extended
by a great set of partners.

01:12:38.966 --> 01:12:41.600
So Google is actually pretty
good, right, about being a

01:12:41.600 --> 01:12:43.000
data-driven company.

01:12:43.000 --> 01:12:45.766
But to support that,
it was all about Dremel.

01:12:45.766 --> 01:12:49.066
Dremel was the purpose-built
analytics engine inside of

01:12:49.066 --> 01:12:51.166
Google that we depend on.

01:12:51.166 --> 01:12:53.100
There is a white
paper out on it.

01:12:53.100 --> 01:12:57.199
It surfaced as Google Cloud
BigQuery for users outside

01:12:57.199 --> 01:12:58.266
of Google.

01:12:58.266 --> 01:13:01.566
It's great because it scales
horizontally in realtime.

01:13:01.566 --> 01:13:03.933
It can ingest millions of rows.

01:13:03.933 --> 01:13:06.800
It can process
trillions of rows a second.

01:13:06.800 --> 01:13:10.166
And you really need that for a
lot of new applications to have

01:13:10.166 --> 01:13:11.199
realtime decision-making.

01:13:11.199 --> 01:13:13.466
Think about like what's
happening with data ingestion

01:13:13.466 --> 01:13:15.766
and IoT and social.

01:13:15.766 --> 01:13:19.166
So it's great for realtime
results across ever-changing

01:13:19.166 --> 01:13:21.433
data sets.

01:13:21.433 --> 01:13:24.933
As part of that, it's a big part
of our data analytics platform.

01:13:25.966 --> 01:13:27.866
What we've been doing is we've
been actually connecting this

01:13:27.866 --> 01:13:32.699
sophisticated data analysis
that's inside of BigQuery with

01:13:32.699 --> 01:13:35.866
rich data sources such
as advertising platforms.

01:13:35.866 --> 01:13:39.266
So today, to make that easier,
we're now seeing the Google

01:13:39.266 --> 01:13:41.466
BigQuery Data Transfer Service.

01:13:41.466 --> 01:13:46.199
What that does is it automates
the transfer of data from SAS

01:13:46.199 --> 01:13:49.300
applications into BigQuery
but it does it on a scheduled,

01:13:49.300 --> 01:13:51.800
managed basis.

01:13:51.800 --> 01:13:54.633
So today we have
connectors for AdWords,

01:13:54.633 --> 01:13:57.066
DoubleClick, YouTube Analytics.

01:13:57.066 --> 01:13:59.733
And then once the data is
inside of BigQuery, that's where

01:13:59.733 --> 01:14:00.933
further enrichment happens.

01:14:00.933 --> 01:14:04.666
You can integrate the ads data
with weather data, geo data,

01:14:04.666 --> 01:14:06.466
your sales data.

01:14:06.466 --> 01:14:09.233
So it makes it really easy
for marketing teams to become

01:14:09.233 --> 01:14:12.566
empowered and they can build
marketing analytics warehouses

01:14:12.566 --> 01:14:13.233
on GCP.

01:14:15.066 --> 01:14:18.033
But the process of actually
bringing data into a data

01:14:18.033 --> 01:14:19.366
warehouse can
still be cumbersome.

01:14:19.366 --> 01:14:22.566
I've seen stats that these data
scientists spend 75% of their

01:14:22.566 --> 01:14:25.566
time just like dealing with
these disparate data sources and

01:14:25.566 --> 01:14:28.366
cleaning them up so that they
can be homogenous enough to

01:14:28.366 --> 01:14:30.466
relate to each other.

01:14:30.466 --> 01:14:32.133
And we want to make that easier.

01:14:32.133 --> 01:14:34.833
That's why today we're
introducing Cloud Dataprep.

01:14:34.833 --> 01:14:38.166
Dataprep is this intelligent
data service that allows you to

01:14:38.166 --> 01:14:41.899
visually explore and clean
your data so that it can be

01:14:41.899 --> 01:14:44.433
integrated into a
BigQuery environment.

01:14:44.433 --> 01:14:46.733
It's as simple as
using a mouse cursor.

01:14:46.733 --> 01:14:50.066
You can hover over an attribute
in a JSON object and you can

01:14:50.066 --> 01:14:52.666
decide that you want that
attribute to be a top level

01:14:52.666 --> 01:14:54.566
column in BigQuery.

01:14:54.566 --> 01:14:57.666
Or we've seen people take a
single field that has a location

01:14:57.666 --> 01:15:01.533
address and the Dataprep service
allows them to break that apart

01:15:01.533 --> 01:15:05.066
and say I want separate fields,
separate columns for state and

01:15:05.066 --> 01:15:06.733
Zip Code.

01:15:06.733 --> 01:15:07.699
And it's really smart.

01:15:07.699 --> 01:15:09.966
It actually uses machine
learning itself, because often

01:15:09.966 --> 01:15:12.466
in data, there is data
quality issues as well.

01:15:12.466 --> 01:15:14.699
And so based on machine
learning, it suggests

01:15:14.699 --> 01:15:17.800
transformations to your
data to make it cleaner.

01:15:20.600 --> 01:15:23.066
So all of these need not
be one time ingestions and

01:15:23.066 --> 01:15:26.300
transformations orchestrated
under a Cloud data flow.

01:15:26.300 --> 01:15:29.399
The aim is that you actually get
out a batch and you're building

01:15:29.399 --> 01:15:33.166
a streaming data
analytics pipeline.

01:15:33.166 --> 01:15:35.966
We've integrated with
Data Studio out of Google's

01:15:35.966 --> 01:15:37.533
analytics team.

01:15:37.533 --> 01:15:40.833
What that now allows is you can
actually visualize, create these

01:15:40.833 --> 01:15:46.066
really rich dashboards and
charts almost automatically.

01:15:46.066 --> 01:15:49.699
Or you can integrate all your
data that's now in Cloud with a

01:15:49.699 --> 01:15:51.233
lot of our machine learning.

01:15:51.233 --> 01:15:53.966
So you can actually train whole
new models and then use them for

01:15:53.966 --> 01:15:56.399
recommendations and predictions.

01:15:56.399 --> 01:15:59.666
Oh, with all of this, never will
you be managing infrastructure.

01:15:59.666 --> 01:16:01.633
You won't be
installing software.

01:16:01.633 --> 01:16:03.899
You won't be
integrating software.

01:16:03.899 --> 01:16:06.566
You won't be managing
performance and scalability.

01:16:06.566 --> 01:16:08.800
You'll just get right
to the mission at hand -

01:16:08.800 --> 01:16:09.966
analyzing data.

01:16:09.966 --> 01:16:11.133
So, enough talk.

01:16:11.133 --> 01:16:14.399
Greg is actually back to live
demo again how all this works.

01:16:18.933 --> 01:16:20.933
&gt;&gt; GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Thanks, Brian.

01:16:20.933 --> 01:16:23.300
This is my last of the
four promised live demos.

01:16:23.300 --> 01:16:26.933
Before we start, I would be
remiss if I didn't thank Chris,

01:16:26.933 --> 01:16:32.733
Neil, Martin, and Robert who
have been helping with building

01:16:32.733 --> 01:16:33.800
these demos.

01:16:33.800 --> 01:16:35.766
Would you thank them for me
before we get too far into this?

01:16:37.300 --> 01:16:39.366
Thank you.

01:16:39.366 --> 01:16:41.366
So to give you a picture of
how this data platform puts

01:16:41.366 --> 01:16:44.366
together, we're going to use
a scenario of an advertising

01:16:44.366 --> 01:16:47.466
agency that puts ads in the back
of those screens in New York

01:16:47.466 --> 01:16:50.466
City taxis, if ever you've been
in a cab recently in New York.

01:16:50.466 --> 01:16:53.800
There's an ad that plays and
you can interact with it.

01:16:53.800 --> 01:16:55.966
To give you the picture
first, this is a simplified

01:16:55.966 --> 01:16:58.833
architecture of
this data solution.

01:16:58.833 --> 01:17:01.433
The taxis are sending their
position in realtime, where they

01:17:01.433 --> 01:17:04.233
are in the city, as well as
their destination, how many

01:17:04.233 --> 01:17:06.933
passengers they have, and
what sort of interactions the

01:17:06.933 --> 01:17:08.566
customers are doing.

01:17:08.566 --> 01:17:10.600
Pub/Sub is then
ingesting that data.

01:17:10.600 --> 01:17:13.699
We're using dataflow to process
a data pipeline to handle and

01:17:13.699 --> 01:17:15.600
process all that data.

01:17:15.600 --> 01:17:18.899
We're also archiving the entire
historical data in BigQuery

01:17:18.899 --> 01:17:22.800
which is a really powerful tool
for you to do ad hoc analysis of

01:17:22.800 --> 01:17:24.766
all of your historical data.

01:17:24.766 --> 01:17:26.800
And at the end, we want
to make a visualization.

01:17:28.966 --> 01:17:33.399
So why don't we actually
see the pipeline in action?

01:17:33.399 --> 01:17:35.633
So this is the dataflow
visualization tool.

01:17:35.633 --> 01:17:36.733
You see Robert has hovered.

01:17:36.733 --> 01:17:41.333
We are ingesting a little over
15,000 new elements per second

01:17:41.333 --> 01:17:44.333
into this database of
all of this information.

01:17:44.333 --> 01:17:46.300
But how do you
visualize all that?

01:17:46.300 --> 01:17:49.466
Google Maps is super powerful
but I would hate to point 15,000

01:17:49.466 --> 01:17:52.100
updates per second
at my Chrome browser.

01:17:52.100 --> 01:17:56.500
So dataflow actually allows us
to down sample that event stream

01:17:56.500 --> 01:17:59.533
to give us a realtime
visualization of taxi positions.

01:17:59.533 --> 01:18:01.166
This is that visualization.

01:18:01.166 --> 01:18:03.966
This is showing where all these
taxis are in New York City at

01:18:03.966 --> 01:18:04.699
any given time.

01:18:05.899 --> 01:18:08.733
Now what if I want to look at
one particular customer who has

01:18:08.733 --> 01:18:10.800
placed ads with us,
a particular agency?

01:18:10.800 --> 01:18:14.933
We can filter that dataflow
and now the visualization will

01:18:14.933 --> 01:18:18.733
update so that we're only seeing
ads that are being placed for a

01:18:18.733 --> 01:18:19.899
specific store.

01:18:19.899 --> 01:18:22.033
In this case, the Acme store,
it looks like, is on the Upper

01:18:22.033 --> 01:18:23.399
East Side.

01:18:23.399 --> 01:18:25.733
And sure enough, we see that
we're placing ads roughly

01:18:25.733 --> 01:18:28.000
geographically near the store.

01:18:28.000 --> 01:18:30.966
But right now we're just
using a dumb rule for this.

01:18:30.966 --> 01:18:32.066
It's just a very simple rule.

01:18:32.066 --> 01:18:32.733
Where is the store?

01:18:32.733 --> 01:18:33.399
Where is the taxi?

01:18:33.399 --> 01:18:34.500
Place the ads.

01:18:34.899 --> 01:18:36.500
Can we do better?

01:18:36.500 --> 01:18:39.033
Can we automate this to start
to take into account all the

01:18:39.033 --> 01:18:42.333
various data that
we have about this?

01:18:42.333 --> 01:18:46.800
Let's start by using BigQuery
to sort of explore this dataset.

01:18:46.800 --> 01:18:51.533
The query Robert has here looks
at all of the taxis coming from

01:18:51.533 --> 01:18:54.533
the airports and tries to see
how many of them are traveling

01:18:54.533 --> 01:18:56.266
near restaurants.

01:18:56.266 --> 01:18:59.399
Now note here he's querying this
historical data even as we're

01:18:59.399 --> 01:19:02.866
ingesting new data at
15,000 events per second.

01:19:02.866 --> 01:19:05.000
So BigQuery gives him the
ability to have an always

01:19:05.000 --> 01:19:08.833
up-to-date realtime
view into his data.

01:19:08.833 --> 01:19:12.266
In this case, we see that
roughly 17% of our taxis are

01:19:12.266 --> 01:19:15.066
from the airport
and near a restaurant.

01:19:15.066 --> 01:19:17.899
That sounds like an insight that
maybe we ought to do some ad

01:19:17.899 --> 01:19:19.100
targeting there.

01:19:19.100 --> 01:19:21.100
And if you look at this query,
you can see it really is a

01:19:21.100 --> 01:19:24.166
non-trivial query and BigQuery
is handling all that for us.

01:19:25.366 --> 01:19:31.133
So right now we are
manually configuring these ads.

01:19:31.133 --> 01:19:34.866
I think what we want to do is
look at a way that we can do

01:19:34.866 --> 01:19:37.366
this in an automatic fashion.

01:19:37.366 --> 01:19:40.566
Now we could similarly search
for every different customer,

01:19:40.566 --> 01:19:42.233
but how do we
build a generalizable and

01:19:42.233 --> 01:19:43.133
flexible model?

01:19:43.133 --> 01:19:45.533
That sounds like a
job for machine learning.

01:19:45.533 --> 01:19:48.199
Machine learning is tailor-made
for taking large chunks of data

01:19:48.199 --> 01:19:51.266
and building models that give
you insights out of that.

01:19:51.266 --> 01:19:53.933
So we're going to build a model
that takes into account all of

01:19:53.933 --> 01:19:54.666
the data we have.

01:19:54.666 --> 01:19:55.766
Where did the taxi start?

01:19:55.766 --> 01:19:56.500
Where did it end?

01:19:56.500 --> 01:19:57.566
How many people were in it?

01:19:57.566 --> 01:19:58.600
What time of day is it?

01:19:58.600 --> 01:19:59.366
Where is it going?

01:20:00.899 --> 01:20:03.133
First, we're going to start
by using, as you see in this

01:20:03.133 --> 01:20:05.699
picture, we're going to use
Dataprep to take all that

01:20:05.699 --> 01:20:08.833
historical data, clean it up,
make sure that it's ready to

01:20:08.833 --> 01:20:11.166
use, and then we're going
to use it with ML Engine.

01:20:11.166 --> 01:20:12.866
So this is Dataprep.

01:20:12.866 --> 01:20:15.600
The first thing to notice is,
across the top, Dataprep has

01:20:15.600 --> 01:20:18.966
automatically inferred
the schema from my table.

01:20:18.966 --> 01:20:20.866
I didn't have to tell it that.

01:20:20.866 --> 01:20:23.600
Now if you look at that
passenger count column, you'll

01:20:23.600 --> 01:20:25.866
notice that it
looks a little funny.

01:20:25.866 --> 01:20:29.533
The histogram up at the top
includes some elements where it

01:20:29.533 --> 01:20:31.666
says there are 10 or 11 people.

01:20:31.666 --> 01:20:34.199
Now I don't know about you, but
I've been in a New York City cab

01:20:34.199 --> 01:20:36.966
and I'm pretty sure you can't
actually put 10 or 11 people in

01:20:36.966 --> 01:20:38.000
a cab.

01:20:38.000 --> 01:20:39.766
So that's a common data bug.

01:20:40.300 --> 01:20:43.466
Dataprep allows me to fix
that in a number of ways.

01:20:43.466 --> 01:20:44.800
I could delete the data.

01:20:44.800 --> 01:20:48.366
I could replace the 11 with a
1 on the theory that it's a fat

01:20:48.366 --> 01:20:49.600
finger mistake.

01:20:49.600 --> 01:20:52.866
And Dataprep allows me to build
simple recipes that clean up my

01:20:52.866 --> 01:20:55.233
entire data at scale.

01:20:55.233 --> 01:20:57.966
It also can do things like if
you look at our location column

01:20:57.966 --> 01:20:59.300
there, it's a composite column.

01:20:59.300 --> 01:21:02.133
It's longitude and
latitude comma separated.

01:21:02.133 --> 01:21:04.466
I would really like to
have a latitude column and a

01:21:04.466 --> 01:21:06.066
longitude column.

01:21:06.066 --> 01:21:09.100
Dataprep allows me to build a
recipe that splits columns with

01:21:09.100 --> 01:21:12.333
composite data into separate
columns so that I now have a

01:21:12.333 --> 01:21:13.533
better schema to work with.

01:21:14.666 --> 01:21:17.600
Now this just scratches the
surface of how Dataprep can take

01:21:17.600 --> 01:21:21.033
this really tedious job of
preparing data for analytics and

01:21:21.033 --> 01:21:23.966
make it much faster
and must more reliable.

01:21:23.966 --> 01:21:26.500
Once we've done this, machine
learning, as you heard from Faye

01:21:26.500 --> 01:21:30.066
Faye, allows me to build a
model on my laptop, upload it to

01:21:30.066 --> 01:21:32.466
Google, and deploy it at scale.

01:21:32.466 --> 01:21:34.000
Let's see how
that actually works.

01:21:34.000 --> 01:21:36.699
If you scroll down in the
dataflow pipeline, you'll see

01:21:36.699 --> 01:21:39.033
we've added an ML engine call.

01:21:39.033 --> 01:21:42.433
So, now as our taxi data is
coming in, we're calling to

01:21:42.433 --> 01:21:45.333
machine learning with the data
and saying help us choose the

01:21:45.333 --> 01:21:48.666
best ad to target for
this particular user.

01:21:48.666 --> 01:21:51.933
So let's switch back over to the
map with the deployed machine

01:21:51.933 --> 01:21:55.666
learning model and now you'll
see that we start seeing some

01:21:55.666 --> 01:21:57.100
interesting combinations.

01:21:57.100 --> 01:22:00.366
When there is one passenger
coming in towards Midtown, the

01:22:00.366 --> 01:22:04.066
taxi is currently in Chelsea
and it's on its way to Midtown,

01:22:04.066 --> 01:22:06.300
we're presenting an offer for
Manhattan Bagels because it's a

01:22:06.300 --> 01:22:08.699
single person, maybe
he or she wants a bite to eat.

01:22:09.666 --> 01:22:20.000
If we pick a taxi that has more
than one person in it - if you

01:22:20.000 --> 01:22:23.899
have a taxi with more than one
person in it - there it is - in

01:22:23.899 --> 01:22:25.300
this case -
that's still not right.

01:22:25.300 --> 01:22:26.333
That's a one person one.

01:22:26.333 --> 01:22:29.433
But they're heading into a
different location and instead

01:22:29.433 --> 01:22:30.966
we've chosen a winery.

01:22:30.966 --> 01:22:33.266
If you pick a taxi with three
people, the system would

01:22:33.266 --> 01:22:35.800
automatically recommend
discounted tickets to Broadway,

01:22:35.800 --> 01:22:38.233
for example, as multiple
people coming to Midtown in

01:22:38.233 --> 01:22:39.966
the evening.

01:22:39.966 --> 01:22:43.266
So that's Cloud ML making
suggestions based on all our

01:22:43.266 --> 01:22:44.100
available data.

01:22:45.100 --> 01:22:49.333
We used Pub/Sub to ingest a
huge stream of data at scale.

01:22:49.333 --> 01:22:51.899
We used dataflow to build a
pipeline to give us a good

01:22:51.899 --> 01:22:53.866
analytics system.

01:22:53.866 --> 01:22:56.466
We used BigQuery so that
we always had the complete

01:22:56.466 --> 01:22:57.966
historical data available.

01:22:57.966 --> 01:23:00.466
We never had to
deal with sample data.

01:23:00.466 --> 01:23:03.666
And at the end, we used Cloud ML
and Dataprep to build and train

01:23:03.666 --> 01:23:05.233
at scale and model.

01:23:05.233 --> 01:23:07.199
What's important here
is what you didn't see.

01:23:07.199 --> 01:23:09.000
I didn't deploy
a virtual machine.

01:23:09.000 --> 01:23:11.166
I didn't deploy patches.

01:23:11.166 --> 01:23:13.633
I spent my time
actually in the data.

01:23:13.633 --> 01:23:16.899
That's the point of Big Data is
to spend the time in the data

01:23:16.899 --> 01:23:19.533
and not in the machines
that take care of the data.

01:23:19.533 --> 01:23:22.133
That's what Big Data on
the Google platform does.

01:23:22.133 --> 01:23:24.666
So I want to thank you all and
I'll turn it back over to Brian.

01:23:29.433 --> 01:23:31.866
&gt;&gt; BRIAN STEVENS: Thanks, Greg.

01:23:31.866 --> 01:23:35.899
So people that know me know that
I loathe buzzwords, and so I'm

01:23:35.899 --> 01:23:37.699
probably going to offend a few
people in the audience, but

01:23:37.699 --> 01:23:40.100
digital transformation is
absolutely one of the ones that

01:23:40.100 --> 01:23:41.366
I hate.

01:23:41.366 --> 01:23:43.133
I think in part it is because
I've never seen even two people

01:23:43.133 --> 01:23:45.033
even agree on
what it even means.

01:23:45.033 --> 01:23:48.466
But what I do know is this,
is that markets are incredibly

01:23:48.466 --> 01:23:52.366
competitive, each year more so
than past, and the new companies

01:23:52.366 --> 01:23:55.566
that actually take advantage of
new technology without having

01:23:55.566 --> 01:23:59.333
any legacy move really quickly
to either disrupt or create

01:23:59.333 --> 01:24:01.633
whole new lines of business.

01:24:01.633 --> 01:24:04.633
The companies that win are going
to be the ones that shed the

01:24:04.633 --> 01:24:08.800
mundane, take advantage of
state-of-the-art technology, and

01:24:08.800 --> 01:24:12.000
actually put people to work,
their intellectual horsepower,

01:24:12.000 --> 01:24:13.966
and they're creating
new end-user value for

01:24:13.966 --> 01:24:15.433
their customers.

01:24:15.433 --> 01:24:18.366
That's what we want to do at GCP
is enable you on that journey.

01:24:18.366 --> 01:24:21.466
So thank you and it's my
pleasure to introduce Prabhakar

01:24:21.466 --> 01:24:23.399
Raghavan, the leader
of our G Suite team.

01:24:31.000 --> 01:24:32.300
&gt;&gt; PRABHAKAR RAGHAVAN:
Thanks, Brian.

01:24:32.300 --> 01:24:33.266
It's so exciting to be here.

01:24:38.433 --> 01:24:41.733
What is productive work?

01:24:41.733 --> 01:24:46.866
It's a systematic progress from
a huge multitude of choices down

01:24:46.866 --> 01:24:49.566
to a decision or an outcome.

01:24:49.566 --> 01:24:53.966
Whether it's figuring out where
to file the document that I just

01:24:53.966 --> 01:25:01.100
got, choosing the words for my
next email, or at the epic end,

01:25:01.100 --> 01:25:03.600
choosing all the pieces that
come together in a grand

01:25:03.600 --> 01:25:08.800
symphonic work, in every
instance we are going from chaos

01:25:08.800 --> 01:25:11.666
and ambiguity
down to an outcome.

01:25:13.466 --> 01:25:18.699
You will notice that the three
examples I just gave you range

01:25:18.699 --> 01:25:21.233
from the mundane to the sublime.

01:25:21.233 --> 01:25:23.899
The mundane end was where
do I file the document.

01:25:23.899 --> 01:25:26.833
The sublime end was
the grand symphonic work.

01:25:29.366 --> 01:25:31.300
Machines are really, really
good at taking care of the

01:25:31.300 --> 01:25:36.666
humdrum down there, but it
takes humans to do the truly

01:25:36.666 --> 01:25:38.666
creative work.

01:25:39.666 --> 01:25:44.800
At G Suite, we are obsessed with
the idea that computers should

01:25:44.800 --> 01:25:50.466
constantly raise the bar on what
they can get done at the mundane

01:25:50.466 --> 01:25:54.133
so that our users, your
employees, have more and more

01:25:54.133 --> 01:25:56.566
time to focus on
truly creative work.

01:26:00.133 --> 01:26:04.399
In a study of several million of
our Gmail users today, we find

01:26:04.399 --> 01:26:08.399
that one in eight of their
email replies is actually

01:26:08.399 --> 01:26:12.600
machine-generated and they take
those machine-generated replies

01:26:12.600 --> 01:26:16.266
and send them off
and they're good.

01:26:16.266 --> 01:26:18.966
So that's a case where we
are raising the bar on what

01:26:18.966 --> 01:26:23.966
computers couldn't do five years
ago but today they can take care

01:26:23.966 --> 01:26:28.633
of that and leave humans to
focus on truly creative work.

01:26:28.633 --> 01:26:33.300
And so what that means is while
our computers may not have

01:26:33.300 --> 01:26:36.566
written Beethoven's Ninth
Symphony, maybe we could have

01:26:36.566 --> 01:26:40.066
freed him up to write
nine more symphonies.

01:26:44.300 --> 01:26:47.166
At G Suite, this has been an
obsessive pursuit for over

01:26:47.166 --> 01:26:48.966
ten years.

01:26:48.966 --> 01:26:55.699
Today, three of our apps are
on over a billion smart phones.

01:26:55.699 --> 01:27:00.733
We have over three million
paying businesses that use the

01:27:00.733 --> 01:27:02.899
G Suite.

01:27:02.899 --> 01:27:08.566
But I will say this has been
an evolution, a journey for us,

01:27:08.566 --> 01:27:12.766
because we have gone from
applications crafted for

01:27:12.766 --> 01:27:19.500
consumers and then outfitted for
enterprises after the fact to

01:27:19.500 --> 01:27:22.766
having an
enterprise first focus.

01:27:22.766 --> 01:27:24.366
I'm going to give you a
couple of data points for that.

01:27:26.500 --> 01:27:30.533
One thing, we have begun to do
early adopter programs with our

01:27:30.533 --> 01:27:35.466
best enterprise customers so
that we don't just build a

01:27:35.466 --> 01:27:37.266
product and push
it out the door.

01:27:37.266 --> 01:27:40.866
We work closely in the final
months of development with our

01:27:40.866 --> 01:27:43.333
top customers and partners.

01:27:43.333 --> 01:27:47.600
We take their feedback and
refine the product and get it

01:27:47.600 --> 01:27:50.466
just right before we deliver it.

01:27:50.466 --> 01:27:55.466
Here are three examples of
recent early adopter programs

01:27:57.666 --> 01:27:58.899
that we have done.

01:27:58.899 --> 01:28:01.033
I call these out
for a specific reason.

01:28:03.199 --> 01:28:08.166
Each of these apps is
something that was built for

01:28:08.166 --> 01:28:09.100
enterprises only.

01:28:09.100 --> 01:28:13.666
These were not consumer
apps ported to enterprises.

01:28:13.666 --> 01:28:15.000
There is no
consumer pedigree here.

01:28:15.000 --> 01:28:19.266
The Jamboard, App Maker, Google
Cloud Search, every one of these

01:28:19.266 --> 01:28:20.833
is an enterprise-only product.

01:28:20.833 --> 01:28:23.966
You will see the
Jamboard in a bit.

01:28:23.966 --> 01:28:26.000
We don't expect too many
consumers to be buying those.

01:28:28.833 --> 01:28:35.933
Now at the center of productive,
collaborative work is content.

01:28:36.566 --> 01:28:39.699
I am thrilled to share a
statistic with you today.

01:28:39.699 --> 01:28:46.600
Google Drive today has over 800
million monthly active users and

01:28:46.600 --> 01:28:49.066
it's on a tear to
hitting a billion.

01:28:49.066 --> 01:28:53.133
It will soon become Google's
latest billion user product.

01:28:56.199 --> 01:29:01.266
We think of Google Drive as
the premiere personal storage

01:29:01.266 --> 01:29:08.666
solution, but we also see an
opportunity there where it can

01:29:08.666 --> 01:29:12.600
evolve from being personal
storage to serving the needs of

01:29:12.600 --> 01:29:17.233
the enterprise for file sharing.

01:29:17.233 --> 01:29:20.433
And as we go through this
journey, I will be making five

01:29:20.433 --> 01:29:24.766
announcements now to
represent steps along the way.

01:29:27.899 --> 01:29:33.366
First, Team Drives goes into
general availability today.

01:29:40.600 --> 01:29:42.866
As you can tell, this has
been one of the most demanded

01:29:42.866 --> 01:29:45.333
features from our customers.

01:29:45.333 --> 01:29:50.433
It lets teams easily share
content in their drive and

01:29:50.433 --> 01:29:51.633
manage the sharing.

01:29:53.699 --> 01:30:00.033
Now once the content is in a
drive or in a team drive, we

01:30:00.033 --> 01:30:05.300
would like to make sure that the
enterprise needs of compliance,

01:30:05.300 --> 01:30:08.433
of archiving, litigation holds,
e-discovery, all of that stuff

01:30:08.433 --> 01:30:09.500
is taken care of.

01:30:09.500 --> 01:30:13.966
So I am pleased to announce
general availability for Google

01:30:13.966 --> 01:30:15.833
Vault for Drive content.

01:30:15.833 --> 01:30:19.766
So once the content is in the
drive - okay, I see a few people

01:30:19.766 --> 01:30:21.000
that are excited
about that as well.

01:30:21.000 --> 01:30:23.066
Thank you.

01:30:23.066 --> 01:30:26.800
Google Vault for Drive has
arrived, general availability.

01:30:28.500 --> 01:30:29.566
All right, you say.

01:30:29.566 --> 01:30:33.699
But if you're a prospect, you
look at it and go that's all

01:30:33.699 --> 01:30:38.433
very well for content in the
Cloud but most of my content

01:30:38.433 --> 01:30:40.199
sits in on-premise file servers.

01:30:40.199 --> 01:30:42.699
What do I do about that?

01:30:42.699 --> 01:30:45.333
I'm excited to
announce that we are acquiring

01:30:45.333 --> 01:30:48.100
Vancouver-based AppBridge.

01:30:48.100 --> 01:30:52.000
AppBridge is a company
that builds connectors from

01:30:52.000 --> 01:30:55.500
on-premise file servers to
siphon the content up into

01:30:55.500 --> 01:30:56.166
Google Drive.

01:31:03.366 --> 01:31:08.733
The fourth announcement I am
making today around Google Drive

01:31:08.733 --> 01:31:10.266
has to do with the following.

01:31:10.266 --> 01:31:13.600
Once the content is up there,
how do you make it accessible

01:31:13.600 --> 01:31:17.033
and easily manageable for
someone who has got a Windows

01:31:17.033 --> 01:31:20.133
laptop or a Mac?

01:31:20.133 --> 01:31:21.233
No more sync clients.

01:31:21.233 --> 01:31:23.633
No more checkboxing
which files you want to save.

01:31:23.633 --> 01:31:28.300
No more worrying about how much
hard drive space do you have.

01:31:28.300 --> 01:31:31.333
Drive File Stream takes care
of all of that seamlessly and

01:31:31.333 --> 01:31:33.866
obliviously so you don't have
to worry about all of these

01:31:33.866 --> 01:31:38.033
minutiae and can work as if
Google Drive, the entire Cloud,

01:31:38.033 --> 01:31:43.433
is connecting
to your laptop. Great.

01:31:43.433 --> 01:31:48.933
Now once all of that content is
up in the Cloud, it opens up the

01:31:48.933 --> 01:31:51.600
potential for Google's
machine learning magic.

01:31:55.600 --> 01:31:56.733
Think of the following.

01:31:56.733 --> 01:32:00.133
You come to your favorite file
depository and you know there is

01:32:00.133 --> 01:32:03.600
a file you're looking for but
you're scratching your head did

01:32:03.600 --> 01:32:07.166
I create it, did Joe share it
with me, what key words does

01:32:07.166 --> 01:32:08.399
it have?

01:32:08.399 --> 01:32:11.399
In our studies, you spend
something like 40 seconds on

01:32:11.399 --> 01:32:15.600
average trying to figure out the
right filters and restrictions

01:32:15.600 --> 01:32:20.266
and browsing and keywords before
you actually get to the file.

01:32:20.266 --> 01:32:21.166
You shouldn't have to.

01:32:21.166 --> 01:32:23.199
There is one file
you are looking for.

01:32:23.199 --> 01:32:28.100
Google's machine learning magic
will build a predictive model

01:32:28.100 --> 01:32:30.833
for who you are and your
activity and serve up that file

01:32:30.833 --> 01:32:32.600
before you even ask for it.

01:32:32.600 --> 01:32:35.566
We call this Quick Access and
today it is generally available

01:32:35.566 --> 01:32:39.500
for both Drive and Team Drives,
Android, iOS, and the Web.

01:32:42.766 --> 01:32:46.100
So that is the cache of
announcements I had to make

01:32:46.100 --> 01:32:47.533
around Google Drive.

01:32:53.399 --> 01:32:58.800
Now that's all about content,
but really where do the people

01:32:58.800 --> 01:32:59.733
come in here?

01:33:02.399 --> 01:33:05.399
So for this next segment of
announcements, rather than my

01:33:05.399 --> 01:33:09.133
keep talking, we're going to do
a bunch of demos around teams

01:33:09.133 --> 01:33:11.600
and meetings for you.

01:33:11.600 --> 01:33:15.733
I'm going to call on stage my
colleagues Scott Johnston and

01:33:15.733 --> 01:33:17.033
Jonathon Rochelle.

01:33:26.833 --> 01:33:28.399
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: I'm Scott
Johnston and I'm going to show

01:33:28.399 --> 01:33:32.899
you how we rebuilt Google
Hangouts with a focus on making

01:33:32.899 --> 01:33:34.699
teams productive.

01:33:34.699 --> 01:33:37.166
And to do this, we're going to
visit a company called Cloudy

01:33:37.166 --> 01:33:40.300
Coffee which is in the midst
of launching a coffee bean with

01:33:40.300 --> 01:33:43.133
100X the caffeine
of a normal bean.

01:33:43.133 --> 01:33:46.199
This is a bean that I
desperately needed this morning

01:33:46.199 --> 01:33:48.500
and I think will be very
popular in the market.

01:33:49.833 --> 01:33:52.833
We're going to start by looking
at the new Hangouts chat,

01:33:52.833 --> 01:33:55.966
completely rebuilt to be an
intelligent messaging app

01:33:55.966 --> 01:33:57.766
for teams.

01:33:57.766 --> 01:34:01.466
Now, Cloudy and our G Suite
customers already use Hangouts'

01:34:01.466 --> 01:34:05.466
direct messages every day
to keep work moving forward.

01:34:05.466 --> 01:34:09.266
But in the new Hangouts
chat, we've added rooms.

01:34:09.266 --> 01:34:14.000
Rooms are a central place for
team and project discussions.

01:34:14.000 --> 01:34:16.733
To help look at this and help
us walk through this, I've got

01:34:16.733 --> 01:34:18.166
Mandy at the controls.

01:34:18.500 --> 01:34:20.866
&gt;&gt; MANDY: Fired up
and ready to go.

01:34:20.866 --> 01:34:24.033
So I'm going to bring up
Cloudy Coffee Room in the new

01:34:24.033 --> 01:34:24.933
Hangouts chat.

01:34:26.066 --> 01:34:28.100
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: So the first
thing you will notice in this

01:34:28.100 --> 01:34:31.399
room is that discussion,
realtime discussion is threaded,

01:34:31.399 --> 01:34:35.833
and this allows me to separate
the lunch conversation from the

01:34:35.833 --> 01:34:40.466
work and allows teams to dive
deeply into discussion without

01:34:40.466 --> 01:34:43.800
fragmenting other discussions.

01:34:43.800 --> 01:34:46.333
Mandy, why don't we move a bit
lower in this room and look at

01:34:46.333 --> 01:34:47.366
some other stuff?

01:34:47.366 --> 01:34:54.766
So here we see Nicole has posted
a brochure for our launch.

01:34:54.766 --> 01:34:57.000
That brochure is in Docs.

01:34:57.000 --> 01:35:01.399
In the new Hangouts chat, Docs
and Drive are deeply integrated

01:35:01.399 --> 01:35:03.800
and the room manages the
permissions for you, so anybody

01:35:03.800 --> 01:35:07.000
who is a member of the room
now or in the future always has

01:35:07.000 --> 01:35:10.399
access to work with that file.

01:35:10.399 --> 01:35:12.533
So now we have a central
place to discuss project and

01:35:12.533 --> 01:35:14.166
team information.

01:35:14.166 --> 01:35:18.366
We know that search is critical,
and so we have built a powerful

01:35:18.366 --> 01:35:20.833
search directly into
the Hangouts interface.

01:35:22.233 --> 01:35:25.066
&gt;&gt; MANDY: So on top of free text
search, you can also filter by

01:35:25.066 --> 01:35:26.633
people or types.

01:35:26.633 --> 01:35:28.899
So you can always find
the content in your rooms.

01:35:28.899 --> 01:35:30.266
Let me take a look at slides.

01:35:30.266 --> 01:35:33.399
Ah, and there's the sales
forecast that Patrick put in.

01:35:34.600 --> 01:35:36.833
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Perfect.

01:35:36.833 --> 01:35:40.633
Teams work with a myriad of
tools today, and so we have

01:35:40.633 --> 01:35:44.733
created the Hangouts platform
that lets third parties deeply

01:35:44.733 --> 01:35:48.600
integrate with
Hangouts and Team rooms.

01:35:48.600 --> 01:35:51.800
The platform supports a wide
range of capabilities from

01:35:51.800 --> 01:35:55.633
lightweight scripting with our
Google Apps script so you can

01:35:55.633 --> 01:35:59.000
automate team workflows
quickly all the way through to

01:35:59.000 --> 01:36:01.133
intelligent bots.

01:36:01.133 --> 01:36:02.266
So let's look at an example.

01:36:03.500 --> 01:36:09.366
Cloudy Coffee uses a sauna, a
work tracking product, to stay

01:36:09.366 --> 01:36:12.566
on top of their launches and
know who is working on what

01:36:12.566 --> 01:36:13.766
and when.

01:36:15.433 --> 01:36:16.733
&gt;&gt; MANDY: Okay.

01:36:16.733 --> 01:36:28.433
So right from inside the room, I
can assign a task to Mike, who I

01:36:28.433 --> 01:36:30.466
like to assign all my tasks to.

01:36:30.466 --> 01:36:34.500
And with one simple
click, task is created.

01:36:34.933 --> 01:36:36.100
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Great.

01:36:36.100 --> 01:36:38.733
We've teamed up with a number
of companies like Zendesk,

01:36:38.733 --> 01:36:43.600
ProsperWorks, Box, and more, to
deeply integrate their products

01:36:43.600 --> 01:36:45.600
into the Hangouts platform.

01:36:45.600 --> 01:36:48.500
And let me show you actually how
we're using the platform itself

01:36:48.500 --> 01:36:51.166
to integrate our own products.

01:36:51.166 --> 01:36:54.833
We've built an
intelligent bot we call Meet.

01:36:54.833 --> 01:36:58.366
Meet uses natural language
processing and machine learning

01:36:58.366 --> 01:37:00.466
to automatically schedule
meetings for your team.

01:37:01.233 --> 01:37:03.533
&gt;&gt; MANDY: I'll ask Meet to find
a time for us - for the people

01:37:03.533 --> 01:37:04.500
in the room today.

01:37:04.500 --> 01:37:05.500
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Perfect.

01:37:05.500 --> 01:37:08.133
&gt;&gt; MANDY: Oops, maybe I'm -
I may have had too much of

01:37:08.133 --> 01:37:09.000
that coffee.

01:37:09.000 --> 01:37:09.433
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Too
much of the coffee? Yeah.

01:37:16.666 --> 01:37:17.533
&gt;&gt; MANDY: Oops.

01:37:24.633 --> 01:37:26.366
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: So what's
happening here is Meet is going

01:37:26.366 --> 01:37:28.899
to go out, it's going to look at
all our calendars for members in

01:37:28.899 --> 01:37:31.733
the room, it's going to find the
optimal time for us to meet this

01:37:31.733 --> 01:37:35.533
afternoon, and automatically
book it in Google Calendar.

01:37:35.533 --> 01:37:38.233
&gt;&gt; MANDY: Actually, let's
move that meeting to tomorrow.

01:37:38.233 --> 01:37:40.433
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Okay.

01:37:40.433 --> 01:37:42.966
So here we are seeing with
simple conversational commands

01:37:42.966 --> 01:37:45.100
we can do something that
otherwise used to take many,

01:37:45.100 --> 01:37:47.266
many steps.

01:37:47.266 --> 01:37:50.133
Wait, if you're moving that
meeting to tomorrow, that means

01:37:50.133 --> 01:37:53.166
all of you are going to have
to stay until tomorrow for the

01:37:53.166 --> 01:37:54.366
demo. Is that okay?

01:37:54.366 --> 01:37:55.733
Are you guys good with that?

01:37:55.733 --> 01:37:56.833
I can lead a sing-along.

01:37:56.833 --> 01:37:58.399
We have snacks. No?

01:37:58.399 --> 01:37:59.199
&gt;&gt; MANDY: Good point, Scott.

01:37:59.199 --> 01:38:00.466
Let me just move
that to right now.

01:38:00.466 --> 01:38:01.433
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Okay.

01:38:01.433 --> 01:38:03.399
So we're going to ask Meet
to schedule a team meeting

01:38:03.399 --> 01:38:04.933
right now.

01:38:04.933 --> 01:38:07.899
And before we jump into this
meeting, let me talk about

01:38:07.899 --> 01:38:09.166
meeting technology today.

01:38:09.166 --> 01:38:13.000
I look around and I see us
landing rockets on rafts in the

01:38:13.000 --> 01:38:17.766
ocean and it's still so hard
to get people into a meeting.

01:38:17.766 --> 01:38:19.699
Our customers sometimes
spend ten minutes getting a

01:38:19.699 --> 01:38:20.800
meeting ready.

01:38:20.800 --> 01:38:24.066
Somebody doesn't have an
account, the meeting system sent

01:38:24.066 --> 01:38:27.133
them 72 phone numbers and
you can't find the code.

01:38:27.133 --> 01:38:29.666
And so this is what we obsessed
about when we rebuilt the

01:38:29.666 --> 01:38:31.366
Hangouts meeting experience.

01:38:32.733 --> 01:38:34.233
What does it mean
to obsess about it?

01:38:34.233 --> 01:38:35.866
It means no plug-ins required.

01:38:35.866 --> 01:38:37.666
One click and you're in.

01:38:37.666 --> 01:38:42.333
We dramatically reduced the
code size and optimized the

01:38:42.333 --> 01:38:45.366
experience so that you're
instantly in the meeting, you

01:38:45.366 --> 01:38:49.233
have less CPU fan, and your
video and audio quality are

01:38:49.233 --> 01:38:51.666
dramatically improved.

01:38:51.666 --> 01:38:53.366
All right, I'm
done with my rant.

01:38:53.366 --> 01:38:54.166
&gt;&gt; MANDY: Great.

01:38:54.166 --> 01:38:55.766
I think we have the
entire team already one.

01:38:55.766 --> 01:38:57.133
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON:
All right, great.

01:38:57.133 --> 01:38:58.933
&gt;&gt; MANDY: So when you click in,
you're instantly taken to what

01:38:58.933 --> 01:39:01.699
we call the green room where
you can check out and make sure

01:39:01.699 --> 01:39:03.600
you're ready for the
meeting and then join.

01:39:06.433 --> 01:39:07.066
Hey, guys.

01:39:07.066 --> 01:39:08.800
Welcome to the keynote.

01:39:08.800 --> 01:39:09.533
&gt;&gt; TEAM: Hey.

01:39:11.166 --> 01:39:12.733
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Here we are.

01:39:12.733 --> 01:39:15.399
So what you're seeing here
is the new Hangouts Meet, our

01:39:15.399 --> 01:39:18.933
enterprise solution
for video meetings.

01:39:18.933 --> 01:39:21.100
So, sure, it loads fast.

01:39:21.100 --> 01:39:22.266
All right, yeah.

01:39:25.800 --> 01:39:28.300
So it loads fast, it performs
well, and I'm sure you'll get to

01:39:28.300 --> 01:39:30.899
try that when we
launch it today.

01:39:30.899 --> 01:39:33.933
But there's also friction
getting into meetings in

01:39:33.933 --> 01:39:35.000
other ways.

01:39:35.000 --> 01:39:37.899
What about that consultant that
doesn't work for the company,

01:39:37.899 --> 01:39:39.366
doesn't use G Suite?

01:39:39.366 --> 01:39:41.166
I don't know why you wouldn't
use G Suite, but some people

01:39:41.166 --> 01:39:42.566
don't I hear.

01:39:42.566 --> 01:39:43.833
No problem.

01:39:43.833 --> 01:39:46.466
Vroon is a consultant that is
helping us with this coffee

01:39:46.466 --> 01:39:49.266
campaign, and with a
single link he has jumped in.

01:39:50.166 --> 01:39:51.766
&gt;&gt; MANDY: So I've just
accepted Vroon's knock and he's

01:39:51.766 --> 01:39:53.166
instantly there.

01:39:53.166 --> 01:39:54.566
Hi, Vroon.

01:39:54.566 --> 01:39:56.533
It also looks like Mike
has joined from the road.

01:39:57.366 --> 01:40:00.100
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: And that's
because Hangouts Meet with every

01:40:00.100 --> 01:40:03.800
meeting now can contain a dial
in code so that you can connect

01:40:03.800 --> 01:40:06.233
and participate in the meeting
even when you don't have a

01:40:06.233 --> 01:40:06.966
data connection.

01:40:12.800 --> 01:40:15.333
So there is a lot more I could
talk about with Hangouts Meet,

01:40:15.333 --> 01:40:18.833
but what I want to stress is
that the focus was to really,

01:40:18.833 --> 01:40:21.399
really get you into the meeting
as quickly as possible so the

01:40:21.399 --> 01:40:23.433
technology would get out of
the way and you could focus on

01:40:23.433 --> 01:40:24.699
real work.

01:40:24.699 --> 01:40:26.333
I don't want to spend more
time taking because we're in

01:40:26.333 --> 01:40:28.766
a meeting.

01:40:28.766 --> 01:40:30.899
So now that we're actually in
the meeting, why don't we talk

01:40:30.899 --> 01:40:32.533
about the ad
campaign for this new bean?

01:40:33.966 --> 01:40:35.633
&gt;&gt; FEMALE SPEAKER: Yeah, I have
some ideas that I can white

01:40:35.633 --> 01:40:37.433
board but I don't know if you
guys are going to be able to

01:40:37.433 --> 01:40:38.199
see it.

01:40:38.199 --> 01:40:39.100
&gt;&gt; JONATHAN ROCHELLE: Stop.

01:40:39.100 --> 01:40:41.000
Please stop.

01:40:41.000 --> 01:40:42.366
You said white board?

01:40:42.366 --> 01:40:43.433
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Yeah,
is there a problem?

01:40:43.433 --> 01:40:44.066
&gt;&gt; JONATHAN ROCHELLE: And you're
trying to reduce friction in

01:40:44.066 --> 01:40:45.033
the meeting?

01:40:45.033 --> 01:40:45.866
That's a great idea.

01:40:45.866 --> 01:40:46.566
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON:
What's the issue?

01:40:46.566 --> 01:40:47.333
&gt;&gt; JONATHAN
ROCHELLE: No, really.

01:40:47.333 --> 01:40:48.600
I mean, it's 2017.

01:40:48.600 --> 01:40:49.899
Nobody's going to
be able to see it.

01:40:49.899 --> 01:40:52.600
And a five person
meeting is bad enough.

01:40:52.600 --> 01:40:55.166
We've got 5,000 people here;
they're not going to be able to

01:40:55.166 --> 01:40:56.399
see the white board.

01:40:56.399 --> 01:40:57.500
Oh, wait.

01:40:57.500 --> 01:40:59.600
Actually, we can just have you
point the camera to it, right?

01:40:59.600 --> 01:41:00.533
Yeah, we'll do that.

01:41:00.533 --> 01:41:01.366
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Yeah.

01:41:01.366 --> 01:41:02.300
&gt;&gt; JONATHAN ROCHELLE:
And you can't save it.

01:41:02.300 --> 01:41:03.100
There's no way you're going
to be able to save it.

01:41:03.100 --> 01:41:04.066
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Can't
I write don't erase?

01:41:04.066 --> 01:41:05.000
&gt;&gt; JONATHAN ROCHELLE:
Yeah, how about that?

01:41:05.000 --> 01:41:05.966
Write do not erase on it.

01:41:05.966 --> 01:41:06.800
That will work.

01:41:06.800 --> 01:41:07.566
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: Let me guess.

01:41:07.566 --> 01:41:08.633
&gt;&gt; JONATHAN ROCHELLE:
Or snap a picture.

01:41:08.633 --> 01:41:09.733
&gt;&gt; SCOTT JOHNSTON: This thing
they rolled out here, do you

01:41:09.733 --> 01:41:10.800
have a better solution?

01:41:10.800 --> 01:41:12.199
&gt;&gt; JONATHAN ROCHELLE:
I think we might. Yeah.

01:41:12.199 --> 01:41:14.033
Let's talk about that.

01:41:14.033 --> 01:41:17.066
So what we need is not to waste
the time once we're in the

01:41:17.066 --> 01:41:17.666
meeting, right?

01:41:17.666 --> 01:41:18.566
We got there really easily.

01:41:18.566 --> 01:41:19.466
Let's not waste that.

01:41:19.466 --> 01:41:20.966
Let's get work done.

01:41:20.966 --> 01:41:23.366
That's why we meet, to
collaborate and to work.

01:41:23.366 --> 01:41:25.466
But there's never been
a great tool for that.

01:41:25.466 --> 01:41:26.566
The white board is close.

01:41:26.566 --> 01:41:29.399
What we need is all the
virtues of the white board.

01:41:29.399 --> 01:41:34.533
It's fast, it's simple, and it's
freeform, but we want that with

01:41:34.533 --> 01:41:37.366
something where all
the friction is removed.

01:41:37.366 --> 01:41:38.633
That's why we created Jamboard.

01:41:40.433 --> 01:41:44.566
Jamboard is a white board in the
Cloud, in the meeting room, and

01:41:44.566 --> 01:41:46.399
beyond your meeting rooms.

01:41:46.399 --> 01:41:48.899
You just pick up the
stylus and you start thinking,

01:41:48.899 --> 01:41:51.233
communicating, and
working with your team.

01:41:51.233 --> 01:41:53.000
So let's see what
that really means.

01:41:53.000 --> 01:41:54.199
So T.J. is going to write some

01:41:54.199 --> 01:41:55.733
ideas that we've
talked about for this

01:41:55.733 --> 01:42:00.433
new Cloudy Coffee
ad campaign, but T.J.

01:42:00.433 --> 01:42:02.433
won't be working alone here.

01:42:02.433 --> 01:42:03.333
&gt;&gt; FEMALE SPEAKER: Hey, guys.

01:42:03.333 --> 01:42:05.366
Now we can't see the Jamboard.

01:42:05.366 --> 01:42:06.300
&gt;&gt; JONATHAN ROCHELLE: All right.

01:42:06.300 --> 01:42:07.933
I think we've got a
solution for that, though.

01:42:07.933 --> 01:42:09.399
So wait for it.

01:42:09.399 --> 01:42:11.466
The Jamboard knows that there
is a meeting going on and

01:42:11.466 --> 01:42:15.666
automatically presents a
prompt, and with one click T.J.

01:42:15.666 --> 01:42:19.033
can join the meeting and present
the Jamboard to the meeting.

01:42:19.033 --> 01:42:21.600
So everybody remote, everybody
on the meeting can see what he's

01:42:21.600 --> 01:42:25.500
doing on the Jamboard.

01:42:25.500 --> 01:42:26.666
Hey, Patrick.

01:42:30.933 --> 01:42:33.533
Patrick, you guys can see
the Jamboard now, right?

01:42:33.533 --> 01:42:34.233
&gt;&gt; PATRICK: Yeah.

01:42:34.233 --> 01:42:35.266
&gt;&gt; FEMALE SPEAKER: Yep, thanks.

01:42:35.266 --> 01:42:36.366
&gt;&gt; MALE SPEAKER: We're all good.

01:42:36.366 --> 01:42:38.100
&gt;&gt; JONATHAN ROCHELLE: And by the
way, we have an opinion on the

01:42:38.100 --> 01:42:39.800
stylus that T.J. is using.

01:42:39.800 --> 01:42:42.300
The Jamboard stylus is passive.

01:42:42.300 --> 01:42:43.466
You don't have to charge it.

01:42:43.466 --> 01:42:44.766
You don't have to pair it.

01:42:44.766 --> 01:42:46.633
You don't have to dock it.

01:42:46.633 --> 01:42:48.633
And when you lose it, it won't
cost an arm and a leg because

01:42:48.633 --> 01:42:50.100
it's passive.

01:42:50.100 --> 01:42:52.166
It's intuitive and simple.

01:42:52.166 --> 01:42:54.766
Most importantly, the Jamboard
still knows the difference

01:42:54.766 --> 01:42:58.199
between that stylus and your
finger so you can write with the

01:42:58.199 --> 01:43:00.933
stylus and erase with
your finger naturally

01:43:00.933 --> 01:43:01.666
and intuitively.

01:43:07.966 --> 01:43:09.566
So, guys, why don't
you help us out here?

01:43:09.566 --> 01:43:11.000
Help T.J. out and take a minute

01:43:11.000 --> 01:43:13.066
and let's
brainstorm some activity.

01:43:13.066 --> 01:43:15.899
So you know the ad campaign that
they're working on, sometimes

01:43:15.899 --> 01:43:18.266
you use those sticky notes
if you're in the same room.

01:43:18.266 --> 01:43:22.000
So the team is going to use
the Jamboard companion apps and

01:43:22.000 --> 01:43:25.399
anyone on the team can now
add content and help T.J.

01:43:25.399 --> 01:43:26.500
with his brainstorming.

01:43:26.500 --> 01:43:28.933
So whether it's from their lap
or their phone or their tablet,

01:43:28.933 --> 01:43:32.566
from an Android or an iOS
device, the companion apps let

01:43:32.566 --> 01:43:36.300
them participate and get work
done to add their ideas, to make

01:43:36.300 --> 01:43:38.466
a point, to organize
what is already there.

01:43:38.466 --> 01:43:40.866
It's the same magical
collaboration experience

01:43:40.866 --> 01:43:44.466
hopefully you've gotten
used to in Google Docs.

01:43:44.466 --> 01:43:45.699
Now we're all at
the white board.

01:43:45.699 --> 01:43:49.500
So Lucy in Liverpool or in the
room over there, or Bereen in

01:43:49.500 --> 01:43:52.666
Brazil, or Tae in
Taiwan, this is teamwork.

01:43:52.666 --> 01:43:54.266
This is actual work happening.

01:43:54.266 --> 01:43:57.800
Progress here and now
while we're in this meeting.

01:43:57.800 --> 01:43:58.766
So let's keep working.

01:43:58.766 --> 01:44:00.899
We're looking at where to
target this ad campaign.

01:44:00.899 --> 01:44:03.666
So let's see another feature.

01:44:03.666 --> 01:44:05.166
We're going to look at
a billboard location.

01:44:05.166 --> 01:44:06.100
So how about New York City?

01:44:06.100 --> 01:44:07.033
Let's go big.

01:44:07.033 --> 01:44:09.000
I've seen a lot of
billboards in San Francisco

01:44:09.000 --> 01:44:10.600
this week, actually.

01:44:10.600 --> 01:44:12.600
But we're going to go big
and go in Times Square.

01:44:12.600 --> 01:44:15.533
So the power of the Web, the
power of Google is right there

01:44:15.533 --> 01:44:18.333
for T.J. or anybody using
the companion apps, finding

01:44:18.333 --> 01:44:22.733
relevant, functional, beautiful
information to add to the Jam

01:44:22.733 --> 01:44:24.333
and your Jam comes to life.

01:44:24.333 --> 01:44:25.633
So T.J. is using Search and

01:44:25.633 --> 01:44:28.233
Maps on the
Jamboard to add useful content

01:44:28.233 --> 01:44:32.800
for the team, and Bereen and Tae
and Lucy and others are adding

01:44:32.800 --> 01:44:33.533
other content.

01:44:33.533 --> 01:44:35.133
So they're working on visuals.

01:44:35.133 --> 01:44:36.199
What's the branding?

01:44:36.199 --> 01:44:40.433
What is the branding feel we
want for 100X caffeine coffee?

01:44:40.433 --> 01:44:42.433
I'm afraid to ask.

01:44:43.733 --> 01:44:44.866
So they're all using Search.

01:44:44.866 --> 01:44:47.833
And Jamboard is also
integrated with Google Drive.

01:44:47.833 --> 01:44:49.066
So let's try something else.

01:44:49.800 --> 01:44:52.899
You see somebody actually added
using the companion app a slides

01:44:52.899 --> 01:44:55.100
deck that we're working
on the financial model for

01:44:55.100 --> 01:44:56.733
Cloudy Coffee.

01:44:56.733 --> 01:45:00.399
So spreadsheets, presentations,
and documents, and anything from

01:45:00.399 --> 01:45:04.733
Drive can be added to the
Jamboard from the companion apps

01:45:04.733 --> 01:45:06.466
