01:53:56.566 --> 01:53:59.399
In addition to all the great
innovation, all the great

01:53:59.399 --> 01:54:03.366
innovation you've heard, one of
the conversations that we have

01:54:03.366 --> 01:54:07.133
with the G Suite for the
companies that you work for and

01:54:07.133 --> 01:54:11.500
board members is about how
the Cloud enables innovative

01:54:11.500 --> 01:54:16.633
business models, new ways of
you creating and changing your

01:54:16.633 --> 01:54:20.666
business that you wouldn't
have been able to do before.

01:54:20.666 --> 01:54:25.199
And we think a large part
- a large part of creating

01:54:25.199 --> 01:54:29.600
innovative business models is
actually working on ecosystems.

01:54:30.666 --> 01:54:37.166
In fact, Gartner actually in
2017 CIO agenda talked about how

01:54:37.166 --> 01:54:42.366
some of the top performers
of companies that they track

01:54:42.366 --> 01:54:45.933
actually participate in
many digital ecosystems.

01:54:47.966 --> 01:54:51.966
Interestingly enough, not
all ecosystems are the same.

01:54:51.966 --> 01:54:53.733
There are many different
kinds of ecosystems.

01:54:53.733 --> 01:54:57.100
And generally when we think
about ecosystems, we say, you

01:54:57.100 --> 01:54:59.500
know, it must be
public ecosystems.

01:54:59.500 --> 01:55:02.633
But there are many different
kinds and all of them affect

01:55:02.633 --> 01:55:04.300
innovative business models.

01:55:04.300 --> 01:55:06.433
So let's spend a couple of
minutes talking about them.

01:55:08.266 --> 01:55:10.966
T-Mobile is a great example -

01:55:10.966 --> 01:55:13.433
great example
of an internal ecosystem.

01:55:13.433 --> 01:55:17.300
Cody Sanford, the CIO, has
taken their core components like

01:55:17.300 --> 01:55:21.066
billing, like customer, like
product, and assigned senior

01:55:21.066 --> 01:55:22.866
leaders to them.

01:55:22.866 --> 01:55:28.133
These senior leaders are
responsible for making sure that

01:55:28.133 --> 01:55:32.666
these core components are
available as APIs with SLAs; by

01:55:32.666 --> 01:55:36.366
the way, only for the
internal folks at T-Mobile.

01:55:36.366 --> 01:55:40.866
In addition to making them
available as APIs, they also

01:55:40.866 --> 01:55:44.666
have to modernize the
stack underneath the APIs.

01:55:44.666 --> 01:55:48.500
And so what this is helping
T-Mobile do is deliver products

01:55:48.500 --> 01:55:51.500
and services at a pace that
they have never done before.

01:55:51.500 --> 01:55:54.866
We're going to see so much
more from this team soon.

01:55:56.733 --> 01:56:00.933
ABnet is one of the largest
distributors of electronic

01:56:00.933 --> 01:56:03.433
components and solutions.

01:56:03.433 --> 01:56:05.500
Recently, they wanted
to get into the Cloud

01:56:05.500 --> 01:56:07.266
solutions business.

01:56:07.266 --> 01:56:10.166
They set up a Cloud ecosystem.

01:56:10.166 --> 01:56:15.166
They now have 5,000
resellers - 5,000 resellers.

01:56:15.166 --> 01:56:19.600
And their Cloud business is
growing over 1,000% - at a pace

01:56:19.600 --> 01:56:23.433
of 1,000% year-over-year.

01:56:23.433 --> 01:56:25.600
Industry ecosystems are
actually well understood.

01:56:28.533 --> 01:56:32.199
The stakeholders in healthcare
IT have all come together.

01:56:32.199 --> 01:56:35.699
These are the providers, these
are the pairs, these are the

01:56:35.699 --> 01:56:38.433
device makers, they are all
coming together to create FHIR,

01:56:38.433 --> 01:56:42.966
which is an interoperability
standard to securely exchange

01:56:42.966 --> 01:56:43.899
patient information.

01:56:43.899 --> 01:56:47.033
FHIR is picking up momentum.

01:56:49.533 --> 01:56:52.066
I love the Ticketmaster story.

01:56:52.066 --> 01:56:54.833
Ticketmaster actually has a
billion dollar API internally

01:56:54.833 --> 01:56:58.300
and API that actually processes
over a billion dollars on an

01:56:58.300 --> 01:56:59.766
annual basis.

01:56:59.766 --> 01:57:02.833
The aspirations are to become
the operating system for live

01:57:02.833 --> 01:57:05.300
entertainment worldwide.

01:57:05.300 --> 01:57:07.600
They want to create a
billion dollar business in a

01:57:07.600 --> 01:57:08.633
public ecosystem.

01:57:08.633 --> 01:57:12.233
They want all of you to be
able to use Ticketmaster

01:57:12.233 --> 01:57:16.166
functionality in the apps that
you create; any apps, whether

01:57:16.166 --> 01:57:19.066
it's in your car, whether it's
your mobile device, or any other

01:57:19.066 --> 01:57:21.100
kind of app that you create.

01:57:21.100 --> 01:57:22.566
We think they are
well on their way.

01:57:23.666 --> 01:57:27.800
What's common across all these
ecosystems is they are all

01:57:27.800 --> 01:57:30.466
focused on one common goal.

01:57:30.466 --> 01:57:33.600
That is to create
innovative business models.

01:57:33.600 --> 01:57:37.699
We think to create innovative
business models you need three

01:57:37.699 --> 01:57:38.533
different parts.

01:57:38.533 --> 01:57:40.866
There are three pillars
to creating innovative

01:57:40.866 --> 01:57:41.933
business models.

01:57:41.933 --> 01:57:46.000
You need to create and join
ecosystems, you need to be able

01:57:46.000 --> 01:57:51.766
to connect to apps, to data,
and to devices internally and

01:57:51.766 --> 01:57:55.466
externally, and be able
to leverage all the great

01:57:55.466 --> 01:57:57.899
innovation happening around you.

01:57:57.899 --> 01:58:00.199
So let's spend a couple of
minutes talking about each one

01:58:00.199 --> 01:58:00.866
of these.

01:58:02.500 --> 01:58:05.666
The question that all of you
have to ask and every G Suite

01:58:05.666 --> 01:58:11.133
has to ask, every board member
has to ask, is your enterprise

01:58:11.133 --> 01:58:14.966
ecosystem ready?

01:58:14.966 --> 01:58:17.433
Because the right ecosystem
platform can help your

01:58:17.433 --> 01:58:21.566
enterprise with keeping an
inventory of products or

01:58:21.566 --> 01:58:26.600
solutions if that's what you do,
apply pricing models to these

01:58:26.600 --> 01:58:28.766
products, different
kinds of pricing models,

01:58:28.766 --> 01:58:33.066
consumption-based, many others,
be able to work through multiple

01:58:33.066 --> 01:58:36.933
tiers of partners because it's
going to be B to B to C in some

01:58:36.933 --> 01:58:41.000
cases, so multiple tiers of
partners, and the most important

01:58:41.000 --> 01:58:47.433
thing, to make your product very
easy to discover and easy to buy

01:58:47.433 --> 01:58:50.399
for end-user customers.

01:58:50.399 --> 01:58:57.699
Orbitera is a multi-tier Cloud
ecosystem that helps ISVs and

01:58:57.699 --> 01:59:02.033
enterprises take their
Cloud-ready solutions and help

01:59:02.033 --> 01:59:04.666
them distribute and sell them.

01:59:04.666 --> 01:59:07.500
We've had great momentum
with Orbitera recently.

01:59:07.500 --> 01:59:10.300
We are now over three billion
transactions per month and the

01:59:10.300 --> 01:59:11.300
growth continues.

01:59:13.633 --> 01:59:17.666
APIs are well understood and
everybody knows that it's the

01:59:17.666 --> 01:59:21.466
cornerstone of every digital
transformation happening in any

01:59:21.466 --> 01:59:23.433
and every industry.

01:59:23.433 --> 01:59:24.833
APIs come in different types.

01:59:24.833 --> 01:59:26.566
There are APIs as a service.

01:59:26.566 --> 01:59:29.033
This is how
software talks to software.

01:59:29.033 --> 01:59:32.033
And now with microservices,
you're going to have thousands

01:59:32.033 --> 01:59:34.633
of APIs in your enterprises.

01:59:34.633 --> 01:59:38.500
And then there is
APIs as interactions.

01:59:38.500 --> 01:59:42.100
This is how your software talks
to the physical world, whether

01:59:42.100 --> 01:59:45.699
